window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2024-12-2", "source": "2024年12月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence \"Nowadays, more and more students have realized the importance of self-discipline in their personal growth.\" You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.You should copy the sentence given in quotes at the beginning of your essay.", "sample": "Nowadays, more and more students have realized the importance of self-discipline in their personal growth. In\nan age flooded with short videos and endless distractions, the ability to control oneself has become the very\nfoundation of academic success and character building.\nA vivid example is my roommate Li Ming. When we entered university, he set himself a firm rule: no phone\nbefore finishing the day's study plan. While others drifted from one game to another, his steady self-\nmanagement allowed him to master a difficult programming course and win a national scholarship. His\nexperience convinced me that talent alone rarely decides how far a person goes; it is perseverance and daily\nself-control that truly shape our future.\nTherefore, I firmly believe that cultivating self-discipline deserves our full attention. Rather than blaming the\nenvironment for our failures, we should learn to say no to temptations and stick to our own plans. Only by\nmastering ourselves can we grow into the people we truly wish to become."}, "translation": {"prompt": "洋山港(Yangshan Port)是上海航运中心的重要组成部分,是中国第一个深水港,也是世界上规模最大的深水港之一。经过近20年的发展,洋山港已实现高度自动化。数字技术和人工智能的使用大大减少了用工成本和碳排放。自主研发的码头管理系统可以在百公里之外对大型设备进行远程操控。洋山港看上去一片繁忙,现场却见不到人工操作,而且能够24小时不间断运作。洋山港将不断发展,为把上海建成一个全球航运中心做出更大贡献。", "sample": "Yangshan Port is an important part of the Shanghai shipping center, China's first deep-water port, and one of the largest deep-water ports in the world. After nearly two decades of development, it has achieved a high level of automation. The use of digital technology and artificial intelligence has greatly reduced labor costs and carbon emissions. The self-developed terminal management system can remotely control large equipment from hundreds of kilometers away. The port looks busy, but no manual operation can be seen on site, and it can operate around the clock without interruption. Yangshan Port will continue to develop and make greater contributions to building Shanghai into a global shipping center."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2024-12-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Ever been talking about something important with someone you care about and found the discussion frustrating instead of fruitful? Negative energy can then cast a  __26__  over your efforts to build a shared understanding.\nIn fact collaborative problem-solving conversations require skills. Strong listening skills are essential. Sprinkling in positivity via appreciation,  __27__  smiles, and warm comments helps. So does the essential ability to keep your emotional tone in the calm zone. If the tone of a dialogue heats up and anger erupts, the dialogue  __28__  from collaborative to adversarial, and progress is likely to come to an immediate  __29__  .\nIn addition, effective dialogue generally has another important characteristic: symmetry.\nSymmetry in dialogue refers to the balance of how much each partner talks. Are you each getting equal airtime? When one participant does most of the talking, this asymmetry or lack of balance tends to become annoying to one or both of you. The silent partner  __30__  tires of only listening, while the talker can feel  __31__  . Usually, both would prefer more equal give and take.\nDifferent rates of speech and levels of voice volume can  __32__  symmetry as well. The faster or louder partner can easily begin taking up more airtime. The slower-talking or softer-voiced one can have trouble getting the floor.\nConversations also lose balance when one partner's opinions count more than the other's. One person's input may carry more sway because their style of expression is more  __33__  . One viewpoint may tend to get lost because it is expressed more  __34__  .\nIf either partner fairly consistently  __35__  or evaporates, modifying the pattern will be helpful. The dialogue will feel more productive, and at the same time, the relationship overall is likely to feel more positive.", "matchIntro": "What Is a Super Blood Wolf Moon?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Blue moons, rose moons, supermoons. For some reason all the websites seem to think you really want to know all about these moons. \"Catch This Weekend's AMAZING SUPERMOON,\" one headline will announce. \"The Supermoon Isn't Actually A Big Deal And You're All Ruining Astronomy,\" another will complain."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The latest example is the super blood wolf moon eclipse which sounds like the name of an emotional song I would have performed a modern dance routine to in 9th grade, but is, in fact, an astronomical event set to occur on January 20. As we'll explain below, there is nothing truly mysterious or otherwise special about this moon, and it will not look like a bloody wolf head in the sky or anything wild like that. We get these super lunar events by smashing together all the qualifiers we've historically used to keep track of full moons throughout the year, and in the age of the internet we can get a little too enthusiastic."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Consider this your go-to resource for all moon-gazing news. Here's what you need to know about the latest lunar event."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Look, it's okay if you don't know. There are probably loads of folks who walk around pretending they totally know why that thing in the sky seems to get bigger and smaller at regular intervals but who totally do not."}, {"label": "E", "text": "The moon orbits Earth, and it's tidally locked—that means it always shows us the same face, instead of spinning around like our planet does. That's why you can always see the man on the moon (or the moon rabbit, depending on your cultural preferences) even as it spins around us. But while the moon is big and bright in the sky when it's full, that's only because it's reflecting light from the sun. But the moon is always moving, so it's getting hit with sunlight at different angles. It's invisible to us during the new moon, because our satellite is parked right between us and the sun; the so-called dark side of the moon is lit up like Las Vegas, but the side we can see is in shadow. A full moon happens when the earth is right between the sun and the moon, so sunlight hits the part we can see. And all the other phases are just the transition from one of those extremes to the other."}, {"label": "F", "text": "The moon isn't always exactly the same distance from Earth, because its orbit isn't perfectly circular. We call the closest point perigee(近地点), and the most distant point is apogee(远地点). 2018's closest perigee and most distant apogee both happened in January, and the difference was about 30,000 miles."}, {"label": "G", "text": "The reason you care about this ordinary change in distance is that it turns a moon super. When a full moon happens close to perigee, it's going to look a bit bigger. Honestly, the difference is not that profound, but if you're in a position to photograph the supermoon next to something that shows the slight increase in scale, it can look pretty cool. January's super blood wolf moon eclipse is super because the date lines up with the closest the moon will get to us during January, but the moon won't actually be at its closest for the year until February's supermoon, which you can expect plenty of undue excitement over."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Blood moons only occur during total lunar eclipses (which can happen a few times a year in any given location). When the moon slips through our shadow, we give it a reddish coloring. The moon can also look orange whenever it's rising or setting, or if it hangs low in the horizon all night—the light bouncing off of it has to travel through a thicker atmosphere there, which scatters more blue light away. But you'll probably only see that deep, sinister red during an eclipse."}, {"label": "I", "text": "A lot of headlines about moons are just ridiculous (you do not need to be particularly excited about a blue moon, it just looks like a regular full moon), but you should definitely roll out of bed to look at a blood moon if one is going to be visible in your region, even though they're just lunar eclipses and not evidence of bloody battles between the sky gods."}, {"label": "J", "text": "In March of 2018, we had our second \"blue moon\" of the year, to much acclaim. And while that's not necessarily special in an oh-gosh-get-out-and-look-at-it way, it's certainly special: a blue moon is a nickname for when two full moons fall in the same calendar month, and we hadn't previously had two in one year since 1999. We won't have it happen again until 2037. Astronomer David Chapman explained that this is merely a peculiarity of our calendar; once we stopped doing things based on the moon and started trying to follow the sun and the seasons, we stopped having one reliable full moon per month. The moon cycle is 29.53 days long on average, so in most months we still end up with a single new moon and a single full one. But every once in a while, one month steals a full moon from another. In 2018 (and in 1999, and again in 2037) both January and March stacked full moons on the first and last nights of the month, leaving February in the dark."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Getting two blue moons a year is rare, but we have individual blue moons every few years. Also, fun fact: not actually blue. A moon can indeed take on a moody blue color, but this only happens when particles of just the right size disperse through the sky—and it has nothing to do with the moon's status as \"blue\". Big clouds of ash from volcanic eruptions or fires can do the trick, but it doesn't happen often, and the stars would certainly have to align for two such rare instances to occur at once."}, {"label": "L", "text": "You may have heard that the super special second blue moon of 2018 was also a Paschal moon. This is true! That just means it was the first full moon of spring, which is often used to determine the date of Easter Sunday. All of this is just calendar nonsense and we refuse to go into it further."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Sometimes you'll see a headline that promises a moon with so many qualifiers it makes your head spin. A super blue blood worm moon, perhaps? Or a super blood wolf moon? Lots of websites will tell you that \"wolf moon\" is the traditional name of the first full moon of the year in \"Native American\" cultures, which is kind of a weird thing to claim given that there are 573 registered Tribal Nations in the US alone today, not to mention historically. The idea that hungry, howling wolves were such a universal constant in January that all of North America with its different cultures, geographies, and languages spontaneously came up with the same nickname is illogical."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Many cultures have traditional names for the full moon in a given month or season, so there's quite a list to draw from if you're trying to really juice up a story on a slightly-bigger-than-average view of the moon. But these are all based on human calendars and activities and folklore; you will not go outside and see a pink moon in April, though I wish it were so."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "With population increases and global urbanisation ever accelerating, much attention is focused on the sustainability of our cities and scarce attention is paid to the countryside.\nRural life is associated with closely-knit communities, sense of belonging, and a simple, tranquil life. Yet whilst some or all of these elements exist in the countryside, so do conservatism and a lack of employment opportunities. And it is mostly due to the latter that rural communities are suffering. Attracted by the improved economics of urban areas, country-dwellers across the world are moving out, contributing to the excessive crowdedness of densely populated cities, and leaving the countryside in desolation.\nSo how can the countryside ensure its survival among these changes; or should it at all? Due to both the distance between residences and facilities and often intermittent public transport, those living in the countryside are heavier automobile users. Rural dwellers also use more energy maintaining their mostly detached buildings. We may be piled up like boxes in the city, but this brings energy efficiency that the countryside cannot match. Rural energy requirements result in higher carbon emissions per person than in the city, so it is actually irresponsible to endorse such a lifestyle choice.\nProtecting the countryside is a hot topic. Those who have made their fortunes in the city often buy second homes in the countryside, visiting intermittently for a sample of a supposedly simple life. The side-effect of this is that house prices are pushed up due to increased demand, leaving them unaffordable for local people. A solution has yet to be found for this challenge, for market forces are further forcing residents out of the countryside. For those who believe in rural preservation, this is deplorable.\nWhilst an idealised view of the countryside is common, the reality is that rural life has continuously evolved. Industrialisation brought an increase in divisions of labour, changing social links between rural people. The internet has introduced once foreign cultures into the countryside. Improved travel connections and affordability have made the world more accessible, creating a \"global village\". Considering these changes, surely an evolving countryside is inevitable.\nPerhaps the countryside should also prepare itself for massive population increases in the future. With cities across the world growing rapidly, how will the current urban generation respond to the inevitable social changes of mass urbanisation? Will the longing for a simpler life become stronger, fueling a rural renaissance?\nAs the world continues to urbanise, the voices calling for rural preservation may well grow louder. But change is inevitable. We know that the world is going to see new cities emerging from the dust and current cities growing into megacities, but the future of the countryside is less clear.", "47": "With population increases and global urbanisation ever accelerating, much attention is focused on the sustainability of our cities and scarce attention is paid to the countryside.\nRural life is associated with closely-knit communities, sense of belonging, and a simple, tranquil life. Yet whilst some or all of these elements exist in the countryside, so do conservatism and a lack of employment opportunities. And it is mostly due to the latter that rural communities are suffering. Attracted by the improved economics of urban areas, country-dwellers across the world are moving out, contributing to the excessive crowdedness of densely populated cities, and leaving the countryside in desolation.\nSo how can the countryside ensure its survival among these changes; or should it at all? Due to both the distance between residences and facilities and often intermittent public transport, those living in the countryside are heavier automobile users. Rural dwellers also use more energy maintaining their mostly detached buildings. We may be piled up like boxes in the city, but this brings energy efficiency that the countryside cannot match. Rural energy requirements result in higher carbon emissions per person than in the city, so it is actually irresponsible to endorse such a lifestyle choice.\nProtecting the countryside is a hot topic. Those who have made their fortunes in the city often buy second homes in the countryside, visiting intermittently for a sample of a supposedly simple life. The side-effect of this is that house prices are pushed up due to increased demand, leaving them unaffordable for local people. A solution has yet to be found for this challenge, for market forces are further forcing residents out of the countryside. For those who believe in rural preservation, this is deplorable.\nWhilst an idealised view of the countryside is common, the reality is that rural life has continuously evolved. Industrialisation brought an increase in divisions of labour, changing social links between rural people. The internet has introduced once foreign cultures into the countryside. Improved travel connections and affordability have made the world more accessible, creating a \"global village\". Considering these changes, surely an evolving countryside is inevitable.\nPerhaps the countryside should also prepare itself for massive population increases in the future. With cities across the world growing rapidly, how will the current urban generation respond to the inevitable social changes of mass urbanisation? Will the longing for a simpler life become stronger, fueling a rural renaissance?\nAs the world continues to urbanise, the voices calling for rural preservation may well grow louder. But change is inevitable. We know that the world is going to see new cities emerging from the dust and current cities growing into megacities, but the future of the countryside is less clear.", "48": "With population increases and global urbanisation ever accelerating, much attention is focused on the sustainability of our cities and scarce attention is paid to the countryside.\nRural life is associated with closely-knit communities, sense of belonging, and a simple, tranquil life. Yet whilst some or all of these elements exist in the countryside, so do conservatism and a lack of employment opportunities. And it is mostly due to the latter that rural communities are suffering. Attracted by the improved economics of urban areas, country-dwellers across the world are moving out, contributing to the excessive crowdedness of densely populated cities, and leaving the countryside in desolation.\nSo how can the countryside ensure its survival among these changes; or should it at all? Due to both the distance between residences and facilities and often intermittent public transport, those living in the countryside are heavier automobile users. Rural dwellers also use more energy maintaining their mostly detached buildings. We may be piled up like boxes in the city, but this brings energy efficiency that the countryside cannot match. Rural energy requirements result in higher carbon emissions per person than in the city, so it is actually irresponsible to endorse such a lifestyle choice.\nProtecting the countryside is a hot topic. Those who have made their fortunes in the city often buy second homes in the countryside, visiting intermittently for a sample of a supposedly simple life. The side-effect of this is that house prices are pushed up due to increased demand, leaving them unaffordable for local people. A solution has yet to be found for this challenge, for market forces are further forcing residents out of the countryside. For those who believe in rural preservation, this is deplorable.\nWhilst an idealised view of the countryside is common, the reality is that rural life has continuously evolved. Industrialisation brought an increase in divisions of labour, changing social links between rural people. The internet has introduced once foreign cultures into the countryside. Improved travel connections and affordability have made the world more accessible, creating a \"global village\". Considering these changes, surely an evolving countryside is inevitable.\nPerhaps the countryside should also prepare itself for massive population increases in the future. With cities across the world growing rapidly, how will the current urban generation respond to the inevitable social changes of mass urbanisation? Will the longing for a simpler life become stronger, fueling a rural renaissance?\nAs the world continues to urbanise, the voices calling for rural preservation may well grow louder. But change is inevitable. We know that the world is going to see new cities emerging from the dust and current cities growing into megacities, but the future of the countryside is less clear.", "49": "With population increases and global urbanisation ever accelerating, much attention is focused on the sustainability of our cities and scarce attention is paid to the countryside.\nRural life is associated with closely-knit communities, sense of belonging, and a simple, tranquil life. Yet whilst some or all of these elements exist in the countryside, so do conservatism and a lack of employment opportunities. And it is mostly due to the latter that rural communities are suffering. Attracted by the improved economics of urban areas, country-dwellers across the world are moving out, contributing to the excessive crowdedness of densely populated cities, and leaving the countryside in desolation.\nSo how can the countryside ensure its survival among these changes; or should it at all? Due to both the distance between residences and facilities and often intermittent public transport, those living in the countryside are heavier automobile users. Rural dwellers also use more energy maintaining their mostly detached buildings. We may be piled up like boxes in the city, but this brings energy efficiency that the countryside cannot match. Rural energy requirements result in higher carbon emissions per person than in the city, so it is actually irresponsible to endorse such a lifestyle choice.\nProtecting the countryside is a hot topic. Those who have made their fortunes in the city often buy second homes in the countryside, visiting intermittently for a sample of a supposedly simple life. The side-effect of this is that house prices are pushed up due to increased demand, leaving them unaffordable for local people. A solution has yet to be found for this challenge, for market forces are further forcing residents out of the countryside. For those who believe in rural preservation, this is deplorable.\nWhilst an idealised view of the countryside is common, the reality is that rural life has continuously evolved. Industrialisation brought an increase in divisions of labour, changing social links between rural people. The internet has introduced once foreign cultures into the countryside. Improved travel connections and affordability have made the world more accessible, creating a \"global village\". Considering these changes, surely an evolving countryside is inevitable.\nPerhaps the countryside should also prepare itself for massive population increases in the future. With cities across the world growing rapidly, how will the current urban generation respond to the inevitable social changes of mass urbanisation? Will the longing for a simpler life become stronger, fueling a rural renaissance?\nAs the world continues to urbanise, the voices calling for rural preservation may well grow louder. But change is inevitable. We know that the world is going to see new cities emerging from the dust and current cities growing into megacities, but the future of the countryside is less clear.", "50": "With population increases and global urbanisation ever accelerating, much attention is focused on the sustainability of our cities and scarce attention is paid to the countryside.\nRural life is associated with closely-knit communities, sense of belonging, and a simple, tranquil life. Yet whilst some or all of these elements exist in the countryside, so do conservatism and a lack of employment opportunities. And it is mostly due to the latter that rural communities are suffering. Attracted by the improved economics of urban areas, country-dwellers across the world are moving out, contributing to the excessive crowdedness of densely populated cities, and leaving the countryside in desolation.\nSo how can the countryside ensure its survival among these changes; or should it at all? Due to both the distance between residences and facilities and often intermittent public transport, those living in the countryside are heavier automobile users. Rural dwellers also use more energy maintaining their mostly detached buildings. We may be piled up like boxes in the city, but this brings energy efficiency that the countryside cannot match. Rural energy requirements result in higher carbon emissions per person than in the city, so it is actually irresponsible to endorse such a lifestyle choice.\nProtecting the countryside is a hot topic. Those who have made their fortunes in the city often buy second homes in the countryside, visiting intermittently for a sample of a supposedly simple life. The side-effect of this is that house prices are pushed up due to increased demand, leaving them unaffordable for local people. A solution has yet to be found for this challenge, for market forces are further forcing residents out of the countryside. For those who believe in rural preservation, this is deplorable.\nWhilst an idealised view of the countryside is common, the reality is that rural life has continuously evolved. Industrialisation brought an increase in divisions of labour, changing social links between rural people. The internet has introduced once foreign cultures into the countryside. Improved travel connections and affordability have made the world more accessible, creating a \"global village\". Considering these changes, surely an evolving countryside is inevitable.\nPerhaps the countryside should also prepare itself for massive population increases in the future. With cities across the world growing rapidly, how will the current urban generation respond to the inevitable social changes of mass urbanisation? Will the longing for a simpler life become stronger, fueling a rural renaissance?\nAs the world continues to urbanise, the voices calling for rural preservation may well grow louder. But change is inevitable. We know that the world is going to see new cities emerging from the dust and current cities growing into megacities, but the future of the countryside is less clear.", "51": "Statements, like \"beauty is in the eye of the beholder(观看者)\", are rarely questioned. They've become so embedded in our consciousness that people think it's absurd to think otherwise. It might be useful, however, to at least push back on this assumption because people evaluate environment, situations, and people aesthetically. We may find that we still believe it is correct, but we may also find that there is a lot more to the situation than you suppose.\nA recent study exploring aesthetic taste was published in Cognition. The results from this study show that people agree very much in their aesthetic evaluation of natural objects, but they disagree more about artifacts, or human-made objects.\nThe study found that shared taste was most common for faces and natural landscapes, but least common among works of architecture and art. The hypothesis is the commonly pleasing features, like proportion or symmetry, are at work.\nPreferences for natural scenes might be learned through life experiences. Factors like habitability, safety, and openness might be preferred as people develop. Some of the details of landscapes change, but the basics are more common, e.g., water, open spaces, and signs of care.\nArt and architecture, unlike natural spaces, do not have the same level of exposure. So, people do not have the same level of shared taste.\nIt's possible that the lower amount of agreement in the shared taste of artifacts has to do more with elements of style, rather than \"behavioral consequences\".\nAll of the consequences seem reasonable (or at least possible), but I think there might be an interesting philosophical conclusion that they did not draw. It seems that there is an objective ground to our aesthetic preferences or evaluations. I am not saying this would mean beauty is completely objective, just that there could possibly be general objective principles at work, i.e., beauty is not simply in the eye of the beholder.\nNature exhibits some of the universal aesthetic features, such as radiance, in a common way throughout the world. It's not exactly the same everywhere, but it is common. For example, a sunset is similar enough in different places to warrant almost universal appeal.\nPossible universal principles of beauty—such as proportion, fittingness, radiance, and others—are general categories, which allow for a wide array of embodiments. When people get involved in making artifacts in architecture or art, they apply these very general concepts in unique ways. But the way they applied the principle may not have universal appeal.\nThis is why it's important to experience diverse cultures and their artifacts because it opens us up to different ways of approaching or constructing beauty. And it is always good to question our assumptions.", "52": "Statements, like \"beauty is in the eye of the beholder(观看者)\", are rarely questioned. They've become so embedded in our consciousness that people think it's absurd to think otherwise. It might be useful, however, to at least push back on this assumption because people evaluate environment, situations, and people aesthetically. We may find that we still believe it is correct, but we may also find that there is a lot more to the situation than you suppose.\nA recent study exploring aesthetic taste was published in Cognition. The results from this study show that people agree very much in their aesthetic evaluation of natural objects, but they disagree more about artifacts, or human-made objects.\nThe study found that shared taste was most common for faces and natural landscapes, but least common among works of architecture and art. The hypothesis is the commonly pleasing features, like proportion or symmetry, are at work.\nPreferences for natural scenes might be learned through life experiences. Factors like habitability, safety, and openness might be preferred as people develop. Some of the details of landscapes change, but the basics are more common, e.g., water, open spaces, and signs of care.\nArt and architecture, unlike natural spaces, do not have the same level of exposure. So, people do not have the same level of shared taste.\nIt's possible that the lower amount of agreement in the shared taste of artifacts has to do more with elements of style, rather than \"behavioral consequences\".\nAll of the consequences seem reasonable (or at least possible), but I think there might be an interesting philosophical conclusion that they did not draw. It seems that there is an objective ground to our aesthetic preferences or evaluations. I am not saying this would mean beauty is completely objective, just that there could possibly be general objective principles at work, i.e., beauty is not simply in the eye of the beholder.\nNature exhibits some of the universal aesthetic features, such as radiance, in a common way throughout the world. It's not exactly the same everywhere, but it is common. For example, a sunset is similar enough in different places to warrant almost universal appeal.\nPossible universal principles of beauty—such as proportion, fittingness, radiance, and others—are general categories, which allow for a wide array of embodiments. When people get involved in making artifacts in architecture or art, they apply these very general concepts in unique ways. But the way they applied the principle may not have universal appeal.\nThis is why it's important to experience diverse cultures and their artifacts because it opens us up to different ways of approaching or constructing beauty. And it is always good to question our assumptions.", "53": "Statements, like \"beauty is in the eye of the beholder(观看者)\", are rarely questioned. They've become so embedded in our consciousness that people think it's absurd to think otherwise. It might be useful, however, to at least push back on this assumption because people evaluate environment, situations, and people aesthetically. We may find that we still believe it is correct, but we may also find that there is a lot more to the situation than you suppose.\nA recent study exploring aesthetic taste was published in Cognition. The results from this study show that people agree very much in their aesthetic evaluation of natural objects, but they disagree more about artifacts, or human-made objects.\nThe study found that shared taste was most common for faces and natural landscapes, but least common among works of architecture and art. The hypothesis is the commonly pleasing features, like proportion or symmetry, are at work.\nPreferences for natural scenes might be learned through life experiences. Factors like habitability, safety, and openness might be preferred as people develop. Some of the details of landscapes change, but the basics are more common, e.g., water, open spaces, and signs of care.\nArt and architecture, unlike natural spaces, do not have the same level of exposure. So, people do not have the same level of shared taste.\nIt's possible that the lower amount of agreement in the shared taste of artifacts has to do more with elements of style, rather than \"behavioral consequences\".\nAll of the consequences seem reasonable (or at least possible), but I think there might be an interesting philosophical conclusion that they did not draw. It seems that there is an objective ground to our aesthetic preferences or evaluations. I am not saying this would mean beauty is completely objective, just that there could possibly be general objective principles at work, i.e., beauty is not simply in the eye of the beholder.\nNature exhibits some of the universal aesthetic features, such as radiance, in a common way throughout the world. It's not exactly the same everywhere, but it is common. For example, a sunset is similar enough in different places to warrant almost universal appeal.\nPossible universal principles of beauty—such as proportion, fittingness, radiance, and others—are general categories, which allow for a wide array of embodiments. When people get involved in making artifacts in architecture or art, they apply these very general concepts in unique ways. But the way they applied the principle may not have universal appeal.\nThis is why it's important to experience diverse cultures and their artifacts because it opens us up to different ways of approaching or constructing beauty. And it is always good to question our assumptions.", "54": "Statements, like \"beauty is in the eye of the beholder(观看者)\", are rarely questioned. They've become so embedded in our consciousness that people think it's absurd to think otherwise. It might be useful, however, to at least push back on this assumption because people evaluate environment, situations, and people aesthetically. We may find that we still believe it is correct, but we may also find that there is a lot more to the situation than you suppose.\nA recent study exploring aesthetic taste was published in Cognition. The results from this study show that people agree very much in their aesthetic evaluation of natural objects, but they disagree more about artifacts, or human-made objects.\nThe study found that shared taste was most common for faces and natural landscapes, but least common among works of architecture and art. The hypothesis is the commonly pleasing features, like proportion or symmetry, are at work.\nPreferences for natural scenes might be learned through life experiences. Factors like habitability, safety, and openness might be preferred as people develop. Some of the details of landscapes change, but the basics are more common, e.g., water, open spaces, and signs of care.\nArt and architecture, unlike natural spaces, do not have the same level of exposure. So, people do not have the same level of shared taste.\nIt's possible that the lower amount of agreement in the shared taste of artifacts has to do more with elements of style, rather than \"behavioral consequences\".\nAll of the consequences seem reasonable (or at least possible), but I think there might be an interesting philosophical conclusion that they did not draw. It seems that there is an objective ground to our aesthetic preferences or evaluations. I am not saying this would mean beauty is completely objective, just that there could possibly be general objective principles at work, i.e., beauty is not simply in the eye of the beholder.\nNature exhibits some of the universal aesthetic features, such as radiance, in a common way throughout the world. It's not exactly the same everywhere, but it is common. For example, a sunset is similar enough in different places to warrant almost universal appeal.\nPossible universal principles of beauty—such as proportion, fittingness, radiance, and others—are general categories, which allow for a wide array of embodiments. When people get involved in making artifacts in architecture or art, they apply these very general concepts in unique ways. But the way they applied the principle may not have universal appeal.\nThis is why it's important to experience diverse cultures and their artifacts because it opens us up to different ways of approaching or constructing beauty. And it is always good to question our assumptions.", "55": "Statements, like \"beauty is in the eye of the beholder(观看者)\", are rarely questioned. They've become so embedded in our consciousness that people think it's absurd to think otherwise. It might be useful, however, to at least push back on this assumption because people evaluate environment, situations, and people aesthetically. We may find that we still believe it is correct, but we may also find that there is a lot more to the situation than you suppose.\nA recent study exploring aesthetic taste was published in Cognition. The results from this study show that people agree very much in their aesthetic evaluation of natural objects, but they disagree more about artifacts, or human-made objects.\nThe study found that shared taste was most common for faces and natural landscapes, but least common among works of architecture and art. The hypothesis is the commonly pleasing features, like proportion or symmetry, are at work.\nPreferences for natural scenes might be learned through life experiences. Factors like habitability, safety, and openness might be preferred as people develop. Some of the details of landscapes change, but the basics are more common, e.g., water, open spaces, and signs of care.\nArt and architecture, unlike natural spaces, do not have the same level of exposure. So, people do not have the same level of shared taste.\nIt's possible that the lower amount of agreement in the shared taste of artifacts has to do more with elements of style, rather than \"behavioral consequences\".\nAll of the consequences seem reasonable (or at least possible), but I think there might be an interesting philosophical conclusion that they did not draw. It seems that there is an objective ground to our aesthetic preferences or evaluations. I am not saying this would mean beauty is completely objective, just that there could possibly be general objective principles at work, i.e., beauty is not simply in the eye of the beholder.\nNature exhibits some of the universal aesthetic features, such as radiance, in a common way throughout the world. It's not exactly the same everywhere, but it is common. For example, a sunset is similar enough in different places to warrant almost universal appeal.\nPossible universal principles of beauty—such as proportion, fittingness, radiance, and others—are general categories, which allow for a wide array of embodiments. When people get involved in making artifacts in architecture or art, they apply these very general concepts in unique ways. But the way they applied the principle may not have universal appeal.\nThis is why it's important to experience diverse cultures and their artifacts because it opens us up to different ways of approaching or constructing beauty. And it is always good to question our assumptions."}, "questions": [{"id": "2024-12-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What did the man want to discuss with the woman?", "options": ["A) Changing his major.", "B) His family situation.", "C) Revising his graduation thesis.", "D) His passion for Art History."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Changing his major.\n【定位】Well, to get right down to it, I'm having second thoughts about my major.\n嗯，直说吧，我对自己的专业起了动摇 / 想重新考虑了。\n【信号】开门见山引出语。to get right down to it（直奔主题地说）是典型的点题信号，紧跟其后\n的 I'm having second thoughts about my major 就是他要谈的核心，这正是开篇定主话\n题的落点。\n【替换】having second thoughts about my major ↔ Changing his major.\n动摇=想换\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 With my family situation getting more complicated，但家庭状况只是促使他\n动摇的背景诱因，不是他『想讨论』的主题；听到原词 family situation 就选会上当。\n借⽤原⽂：With my family situation getting more complicated\nC) 原词陷阱\nthesis（毕业论文）确实在后文 thesis is due by the end of this year 被提到，但那是讲他\n没时间的论据，与他开头要谈的『换专业』无关，更没说要 revise（修改）论文。\n借⽤原⽂：thesis is due by the end of this year\nD) 与原文相反\npassion for Art History 出自女方回忆 how passionate you are about the Art History\nprogramme，那是男生过去的热爱；他现在恰恰是要放弃，方向相反，不是他想讨论的\n事。\n借⽤原⽂：how passionate you are about the Art History programme", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "Why does the man want to give up Art History?", "options": ["A) He doesn't think it legitimate to depend on his father.", "B) He thinks his financial situation has now changed.", "C) He doesn't think it will provide him with a living.", "D) He thinks it nourishes the financially secure only."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He doesn't think it will provide him with a living.\n【定位】Art History nourishes my soul, but does nothing to put food on my plate.\n艺术史滋养我的灵魂，却完全没法让我糊口 / 养活自己。\n【信号】转折 but 后是真意。but does nothing to put food on my plate 中的 but 是关键信号：\n前半 nourishes my soul 是让步，转折之后才是他放弃的真正理由——这门专业养不活自\n己。转折后才是落点。\n【替换】does nothing to put food on my plate ↔ He doesn't think it will provide him with a living.\n糊口=谋生\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音出现过 My father's current financial situation 和别处的 legitimate，但他从没说\n『依赖父亲不正当』；这是把不同句的原词拼成一个似是而非的理由。\n借⽤原⽂：My father's current financial situation has helped me realize\nB) 张冠李戴\nMy father's current financial situation 是『父亲的』经济状况让他醒悟，并非『他自己的\n经济状况变了』；选项把主语和含义都改了。\n借⽤原⽂：My father's current financial situation has helped me realize\nD) 偷换概念\nnourishes 确实念到了，但原文是 Art History nourishes my soul——艺术史滋养灵魂是\n他舍不得它的地方，不是放弃的理由；选项把它说成『只滋养经济无虞者』，纯属歪曲。\n借⽤原⽂：Art History nourishes my soul", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the woman advise the man to do?", "options": ["A) Try his best to socialize and build a network in the art industry.", "B) Seek financially viable employment opportunities after graduation.", "C) Investigate all possible ways to become a celebrity in the art world.", "D) Strike a balance between intellectual pursuits and financial security."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Try his best to socialize and build a network in the art industry.\n【定位】if your heart is set on working in the art industry, you need to be more assertive and start\ntalking with people. Networking is key if you want to become a celebrity in the world of\nmuseums and galleries.\n如果你一心想在艺术行业工作，就得更主动些，开始去和人交谈。如果你想成为博物馆和画\n廊界的名人，建立人脉至关重要。\n【信号】建议句 you need to / is key。you need to be more assertive and start talking with\npeople 用 you need to 直接发出建议，Networking is key 又以『…至关重要』强调要点\n——这两处劝告语气正是建议题的答案信号。\n【替换】start talking with people ↔ Try his best to socialize and build a network in the art industry.\n和人交谈=社交\nNetworking is key ↔ build a network in the art industry\nnetworking=build a network\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nfinancially viable employment 是男方在 a more financially viable employment\nopportunity is not necessarily a bad thing 说的想法，不是女方的建议；女方反而劝他坚\n持艺术行业。\n借⽤原⽂：a more financially viable employment opportunity is not necessarily a bad thing\nC) 偷换概念\n录音里 become a celebrity in the world of museums and galleries 是『成名』这一目\n标，女方建议的手段是建人脉；选项把它说成『研究怎样成名』，把目标当成了建议的动\n作。\n借⽤原⽂：become a celebrity in the world of museums and galleries\nD) 未提及\nStrike a balance between intellectual pursuits and financial security. 听上去最稳妥，\n但女方通篇没说『要在理想与现实间平衡』，她是明确劝他坚持艺术、去建人脉；这是录音\n没提及的折中说法。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the man say he really lacks right now?", "options": ["A) Money.", "B) Time.", "C) Determination.", "D) Optimism."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Time.\n【定位】Time is a very precious commodity right now. My social life does not exist. I can barely keep\nup with my studies and thesis is due by the end of this year. When can I find time to research\njobs when I have so much to do as it is?\n时间现在是非常稀缺的东西。我根本没有社交生活，连学业都快跟不上，论文今年底就要\n交。我本就忙得不可开交，哪还能挤出时间去找工作？\n【信号】稀缺信号 precious / barely。Time is a very precious commodity right now 中的\nprecious（珍贵稀缺）直接点出匮乏，后面 I can barely keep up 和 When can I find\ntime 反复诉说时间不够——这一连串稀缺信号都指向时间。\n【替换】Time is a very precious commodity right now ↔ Time.\n稀缺=缺乏\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n金钱话题（financial、food on my plate）出现在前文谈放弃专业时，但他在被问『缺什\n么』的这段里抱怨的是时间不是钱；把别处的 money 话题搬过来就答偏了。\n借⽤原⽂：does nothing to put food on my plate\nC) 未提及 录音没说他缺决心；他缺的是时间，决心与原文无对应。\nD) 原词陷阱\n女方说过 Now is the optimum time，optimum（最佳的）与 optimism（乐观）形近易\n混，但这是女方的话、且讲的是『最佳时机』，与他缺什么无关。\n借⽤原⽂：Now is the optimum time to put forth your best effort", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What are the speakers mainly talking about in the conversation?", "options": ["A) Their decision on investigating beef consumption.", "B) Their original ideas about the domestic market.", "C) Their different approaches to a case study.", "D) Their end-of-semester business projects."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题） Their end-of-semester business projects.\n【定位】M: Have you decided yet what your end-of-semester business project will be on? ... W: I'm\ngoing to design a business case study for a cow farm... M: What are you doing your case\nstudy on? M: I was thinking about a car wash...\n男：你定下来期末商业项目做什么了吗？……女：我打算给一个供应国内市场牛肉的养牛场\n设计一个商业案例……男：你的案例研究做什么？男：我本来在想做洗车……\n【信号】首句点题且全程围绕。开头第一句 what your end-of-semester business project will be\non 就点出话题，之后两人轮流介绍各自的 project（女的牛肉、男的洗车），话题从头贯到\n尾——主旨就靠\"开头点题 + 全程不离\"判定。\n【替换】end-of-semester business project ↔ Their end-of-semester business projects\n几乎原词照搬\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nTheir decision on investigating beef consumption 盗用了录音里 Beef consumption\nhas been rising strongly 的 beef consumption，但那只是女方项目内的一个论据，既\n非\"两人\"共谈、也非全程主旨，以偏概全。\n借⽤原⽂：Beef consumption has been rising strongly over the past 20 years\nB) 原词陷阱\nTheir original ideas about the domestic market 借了 the domestic market 的原词，但\n对话主谈的是\"做什么项目\"，domestic market 只是女方方案里的一个限定，不能代表整\n段。\n借⽤原⽂：supplies beef for the domestic market\nC) 过度推断\nTheir different approaches to a case study 听上去合理，因为两人确实各做 case\nstudy，但录音中两人是\"各做各的不同生意\"，并没有讨论\"做案例的不同方法\"，\napproaches 是无中生有。\n借⽤原⽂：What are you doing your case study on?", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the man say makes perfect sense?", "options": ["A) Expanding farmland out west.", "B) Importing most of the beef.", "C) Raising cattle domestically.", "D) Continuing to boost economic growth."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Raising cattle domestically.\n【定位】M: Of course. Makes perfect sense. There's plenty of land out west. So, if done right, it\nshould be very profitable to raise our own cattle.\n男：当然。完全说得通。西部有大片土地。所以，只要做得对，自己养牛应该很赚钱。\n【信号】题干原词 makes perfect sense。录音里 Makes perfect sense 与题干同词出现，紧跟其\n后的 to raise our own cattle 就是男方认可的做法——题干的关键短语在原文复现处，答\n案就在它周围。\n【替换】to raise our own cattle ↔ Raising cattle domestically\nour own=domestically\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nExpanding farmland out west 盗用了男方 plenty of land out west 的 land out west，\n但他是用\"西部有地\"来论证自养可行，并非主张\"扩张西部农田\"，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：There's plenty of land out west\nB) 与原文相反\nImporting most of the beef 借了女方 most of the beef is imported 的原词，但\nimported 在录音里是\"现状的弊端、要被自养取代\"的对象，绝不是 makes sense 的对象，\n取的恰是反面。\n借⽤原⽂：most of the beef is imported, which brings with it added fees\nD) 原词陷阱\nContinuing to boost economic growth 盗用了 the economy continues to grow 的\neconomy/grow，但经济增长在录音里是\"需求会涨\"的大背景，男方认可的是\"自养\"这个做\n法，不是\"继续推动经济增长\"。\n借⽤原⽂：As the economy continues to grow, consumer demand is expected to continue\nto increase", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "Why does the man believe he has a vast and continual market?", "options": ["A) Technical equipment.", "B) Business consultancy.", "C) Beef.", "D) Carwashing."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Carwashing.\n【定位】W: That's cool. I could see that working. After all, there's plenty of cars about and those aren't\ngoing away any time soon. While many people don't care how their car looks, many other\npeople do. You got yourself a vast and continual market.\n女：这挺好。我看行得通。毕竟到处都是车，而且短期内不会消失。虽然很多人不在乎自己\n的车好不好看，但也有很多人在乎。你有了一个庞大又持续的市场。\n【信号】题干原句 vast and continual market 的上文。录音 You got yourself a vast and\ncontinual market 与题干同句，它前面 there's plenty of cars about 给出原因，而这整段\n是在评价男方的\"洗车\"项目——理由句紧贴结论句。\n【替换】I was thinking about a car wash ↔ Carwashing\ncar wash=Carwashing\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nTechnical equipment 盗用了后文 a bit more technical equipment 的原词，但那是男方\n靠它\"加价\"的卖点，不是\"市场大\"的对象，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：offering just a bit more technical equipment and professional service\nB) 未提及\nBusiness consultancy 在录音里完全没出现，两人谈的是牛肉和洗车两门生意，没有\"商业\n咨询\"这一项，纯属无中生有。\nC) 张冠李戴\nBeef 盗用女方项目里的核心词，但\"vast and continual market\"那句明明针对男方的洗车\n说的，把牛肉安到\"男方的大市场\"上是张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：I'm going to design a business case study for a cow farm that supplies beef", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "Who would the man's imagined business cater to?", "options": ["A) Car owners of all walks of life.", "B) High-end customers in big cities.", "C) Consumers craving for professional service.", "D) Well-off dealers seeking a profitable markup."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） High-end customers in big cities.\n【定位】M: I was thinking of having it cater to high-end consumers in large cities. It could charge a\nprofitable markup by offering just a bit more technical equipment and professional service\nthan competitors.\n男：我本想让它面向大城市的高端消费者。靠比对手多提供一点技术设备和专业服务，就能\n收取可观的利润加价。\n【信号】cater to 直接领出受众。录音 cater to high-end consumers in large cities 里 cater to\n与题干同词，它后面紧跟的\"高端消费者、在大城市\"就是面向的人群——指向受众的动词后\n那一串就是答案。\n【替换】high-end consumers in large cities ↔ High-end customers in big cities\n近义词逐一对应\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nCar owners of all walks of life 用了 car（与洗车相关）勾人，但男方限定的是\"高端、大\n城市\"的窄众，of all walks of life（各行各业）把范围扩成了所有人，与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：cater to high-end consumers in large cities\nC) 原词陷阱\nConsumers craving for professional service 盗用了 professional service 的原词，但专\n业服务是男方\"提供\"的卖点，而非他面向的客户特征，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：offering just a bit more technical equipment and professional service\nD) 张冠李戴\nWell-off dealers seeking a profitable markup 盗用了 a profitable markup 的原词，但\nmarkup（加价获利）是男方\"自己\"要做的事，不是他面向的客户；把商家行为安成客户身\n份，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：It could charge a profitable markup", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What do we learn from a new study about young children?", "options": ["A) Parents' talking with them during TV time raises their curiosity levels.", "B) Their daily television exposure cuts parent-child conversation time.", "C) The more TV they watch the poorer their progress in development.", "D) Their socioeconomic levels impact their academic achievement."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Parents' talking with them during TV time raises their curiosity levels.\n【定位】As a new study suggests, the more parents engaged in conversation with preschoolers during\nTV time, the more likely those children were to have higher curiosity levels when they\nreached kindergarten.\n正如一项新研究所示，家长在看电视时与学龄前儿童交谈得越多，这些孩子上幼儿园时拥有\n更高好奇心水平的可能性就越大。\n【信号】研究结论标志语 a new study suggests。录音用 As a new study suggests 这个明确的\n结论引出语点题，紧跟其后的 the more… the more… 句式正是新研究的核心结论，听到\nstudy suggests 就该锁定其后那句为答案句。\n【替换】the more parents engaged in conversation with preschoolers during TV time, the more likely those\nchildren were to have higher curiosity levels\n↔ Parents' talking with them during TV time raises their curiosity levels\nthe more…the more 改写为 raises\nengaged in conversation with preschoolers during TV time ↔ talking with them during TV time\nengaged in conversation = talking\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 借用了录音第二段念到的 daily television exposure 这个原词，编出看电视会减少亲子对\n话时间的说法，但录音从未说看电视削减对话，反而强调看电视时家长要多陪聊，属于听到\n原词就想选的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：Researchers assessed hours of daily television exposure and frequency of parent-\nscreen-time conversation\nC) 答非所问\nC 用了录音开头 poorer progress in some areas of development 的原词，说成看得越多\n发展越差的递进因果，但录音只说看电视时间增多长期与发展较差相关联，是个背景前提、\n并非本研究的新发现，新发现是陪聊提升好奇心，故 C 答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：Increased television time for young children has long been linked with poorer progress\nin some areas of development.\nD) 偷换概念\nD 借用录音里的 socioeconomic disadvantages 一词，说成经济水平影响学业成就，但录\n音只把社会经济弱势作为陪聊效果尤其明显的人群补充，并未把它当作研究的主结论，属于\n把次要信息偷换成主结论。\n借⽤原⽂：This was particularly true for children with socioeconomic disadvantages.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What did Prachi Shah's study specifically focus on?", "options": ["A) Kids' enhanced learning.", "B) Kids' curiosity levels.", "C) Kids' reading and math.", "D) Kids' behavioral development."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Kids' curiosity levels.\n【定位】The study specifically focused on curiosity levels, which are associated with enhanced\nlearning and higher academic achievement in reading and math at kindergarten, and\nbehavioral-developmental benefits.\n这项研究特别聚焦于好奇心水平，好奇心与幼儿园阶段更强的学习能力、更高的读写与数学\n学业成就，以及行为发展方面的益处相关联。\n【信号】限定词 specifically focused on 直接点聚焦点。录音 The study specifically focused on\ncuriosity levels 里 specifically focused on 把研究范围明确收窄到 curiosity levels 这一\n项，限定词后紧跟的对象就是答案句的核心，听到 focused on 就锁定其后的名词。\n【替换】The study specifically focused on curiosity levels ↔ Kids' curiosity levels\ncuriosity levels 原概念被前置 Kids'\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 用了录音里的原词 enhanced learning，但这是好奇心 associated with（相关联）的好\n处之一，出现在 which 引导的延伸部分，而非研究 focused on 的对象，听到原词就选会落\n入这个陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：which are associated with enhanced learning and higher academic achievement in\nreading and math\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 照搬录音原词 reading and math，但 reading and math 是好奇心相关联的学业成就领\n域，属于延伸好处，不是研究聚焦的对象，同样是听到具体学科词就想选的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：higher academic achievement in reading and math at kindergarten\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 取自录音原词 behavioral-developmental benefits，但这同样是好奇心带来的益处之\n一，与 enhanced learning、reading and math 并列在延伸部分，并非研究 focused on\n的对象，属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：and behavioral-developmental benefits, especially for children from families with lower\nsocioeconomic status", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the passage say about excessive media exposure?", "options": ["A) It can hinder kids from getting on with their peers.", "B) It can cut into kids' time on exploratory activities.", "C) It can arouse kids' interest in how people interact in real life.", "D) It can widen the gap between kids from different economic statuses."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It can cut into kids' time on exploratory activities.\n【定位】Excessive media exposure, including television, can displace exploratory activities such\nas play and parent-child interactions, which are believed to be key to cultivating curiosity\nin kids.\n包括电视在内的过度媒体接触，会挤占诸如游戏和亲子互动这样的探索性活动，而这些活动\n被认为是培养孩子好奇心的关键。\n【信号】主体加负面谓语 can displace。录音第三段开头 Excessive media exposure, including\ntelevision, can displace exploratory activities 中，主体 excessive media exposure 后\n紧跟负面谓语 can displace（挤占），这就是直接回答其后果的答案句，听到题目主体原词\n加一个动词就锁定该句。\n【替换】can displace exploratory activities such as play and parent-child interactions ↔\ncut into kids' time on exploratory activities\ndisplace 改写为 cut into time\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说过度媒体接触会妨碍孩子与同伴相处（get on with their peers），但录音通篇只说挤\n占游戏和亲子互动，从未提到与同伴相处或交友受阻，属录音根本没说的内容。\nC) 与原文相反\nC 说能激发孩子对真实人际互动的兴趣，是正面后果，而录音 can displace exploratory\nactivities such as play and parent-child interactions 借用了 interactions 这个原词却把\n方向说反了：原文是挤占亲子互动这一负面结果，C 却说成激发兴趣，方向完全相反。\n借⽤原⽂：can displace exploratory activities such as play and parent-child interactions\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 借用了录音里反复出现的社会经济地位概念（lower socioeconomic status），说成会拉\n大不同经济地位孩子间的差距，但录音从未说过度媒体接触会扩大经济差距，只把经济弱势\n作为陪聊受益尤其明显的人群，属于听到熟悉术语就想选的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：especially for children from families with lower socioeconomic status", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What is the dark side of our inborn drive to acquire?", "options": ["A) Exerting ourselves too hard in order to attain our life's goals.", "B) Being possessed with a single thought of professional success.", "C) Trying every means to beat others in terms of personal aspirations.", "D) Being mindlessly driven to possess more and more material things."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Being mindlessly driven to possess more and more material things.\n【定位】The \"dark side\" of this drive to acquire is being mindlessly driven to possess more and more\n\"things\" and to socially compare our \"stuff\" with all the stuff other people have in their lives.\n这种获取欲的\"阴暗面\"就是：被盲目地驱使着去占有越来越多的\"东西\"，并把自己的\"物\n品\"和别人生活里拥有的所有物品作社会性比较。\n【信号】题干原词在原文复现。原文 The \"dark side\" of this drive to acquire is 和题干用了同样的\ndark side、drive to acquire，是\"原词定位\"信号——听到题干念到的词，原文这一句就是\n答案句。\n【替换】being mindlessly driven to possess more and more \"things\" ↔\nBeing mindlessly driven to possess more and more material things\nthings 具体化为 material things\n【排除】A) 未提及\nExerting ourselves too hard in order to attain our life's goals. 谈\"为目标过度用力\"，但\n原文 dark side 指的是盲目占有物质，并非\"努力过头\"，属未提及。\nB) 原词陷阱\nBeing possessed with a single thought of professional success. 借了原文后文出现的\nsuccess（succeed in your professional and personal life）来诱选，但那处讲的是\"有意\n识地追求成功\"的正面建议，不是 dark side。\n借⽤原⽂：succeed in your professional and personal life\nC) 过度推断\nTrying every means to beat others in terms of personal aspirations. 说\"想方设法胜过\n别人\"，原文谈的是\"和别人比较自己的物品\"（compare），并非\"击败别人\"，把 compare\n夸大成 beat。\n借⽤原⽂：socially compare our \"stuff\" with all the stuff other people have", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What might happen if we do not harness our inborn drive to acquire?", "options": ["A) We might isolate ourselves from our fellow beings.", "B) We might acquire an incorrect sense of well-being.", "C) We might end up pursuing all the wrong things.", "D) We might make a mess of our personal lives."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） We might end up pursuing all the wrong things.\n【定位】If you're not consciously, mindfully and intentionally harnessing this inborn drive to acquire,\nyou might be mindlessly pursuing all the wrong things.\n如果你不是有意识地、用心地、有目的地去驾驭这种与生俱来的获取欲，你可能就会盲目地\n去追求一切错误的东西。\n【信号】条件句 If…you might 的结果半句。原文 If you're not...harnessing this inborn drive to\nacquire, you might be... 是 If…you might 结构，转折点在逗号后——后半句才是\"会发生\n什么\"的落点。\n【替换】you might be mindlessly pursuing all the wrong things ↔\nWe might end up pursuing all the wrong things\nyou 换 We、加 end up 不改义\n【排除】A) 未提及\nWe might isolate ourselves from our fellow beings. 说\"把自己和同类隔离\"，原文从未\n讲会孤立自己，属凭常识脑补的未提及项。\nB) 原词陷阱\nWe might acquire an incorrect sense of well-being. 借了原文高频词 well-being 来诱\n选，但原文 well-being 都用在\"如何正向利用获取欲、提升幸福感\"，并非\"不驾驭就会得到\n错误的幸福感\"。\n借⽤原⽂：benefit our well-being\nD) 原词陷阱\nWe might make a mess of our personal lives. 借 personal（原文有 personal life）造\n出\"搞砸私人生活\"的后果，但原文只说会\"追错东西\"，没说生活会一团糟。\n借⽤原⽂：succeed in your professional and personal life", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the passage say about our intentional aspirational goals?", "options": ["A) They should be based on solid theoretical concepts.", "B) They should take personal interests into account.", "C) They should include goals to help other people.", "D) They should increase our sense of worthiness."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They should include goals to help other people.\n【定位】While your intentional aspirational goals will include pursuit of acquiring things that will\nincrease your sense of well-being, they ought to also include goals to help other people in\nyour various life roles.\n虽然你那些有意识的人生目标会包含\"去获取能提升幸福感的东西\"这一追求，但它们还应当\n包含\"在你各种人生角色中去帮助他人\"的目标。\n【信号】让步 While + ought to also 的递进落点。原文 While...will include..., they ought to\nalso include goals to help other people 用 While 让步开头、真正主张在 ought to also\n之后——递进\"还应当\"处才是落点。\n【替换】they ought to also include goals to help other people ↔\nThey should include goals to help other people\nought to=should，其余照搬\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey should be based on solid theoretical concepts. 借了原文 theoretical 一词，但原\n文是叫你\"从实践而非理论角度看\"（practical, rather than theoretical），态度恰好相反。\n借⽤原⽂：from a practical, rather than theoretical viewpoint\nB) 偷换概念\nThey should take personal interests into account. 说\"应考虑个人兴趣\"，原文谈的是\npersonal best、personal life，从没说 goals 要照顾\"个人兴趣\"，属偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：striving to be your personal best\nD) 原词陷阱\nThey should increase our sense of worthiness. 仿照原文 increase your sense of well-\nbeing 的句式，把 well-being 换成 worthiness（价值感），但那句讲的是\"获取能提升幸\n福感的东西\"，且不是本题问的\"还应当包含什么\"的落点。\n借⽤原⽂：increase your sense of well-being", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What will you end up doing without pursuing intentional aspirational goals?", "options": ["A) Drifting through life aimlessly.", "B) Giving up the chance to fulfill yourself.", "C) Abandoning all that life has to offer.", "D) Spoiling your character and integrity."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Drifting through life aimlessly.\n【定位】If you have nothing to strive for, you're putting yourself in the dangerous position of simply\ndrifting through life, and you will not be able to experience, live and enjoy all that life has\nto offer.\n如果你没有什么可为之奋斗的，你就把自己置于一个危险的境地——只是漫无目的地随波逐\n流地过一生，而且你将无法去体验、去活出、去享受生活所能给予的一切。\n【信号】文末 dangerous position 的警示结论。原文 you're putting yourself in the dangerous\nposition of simply drifting through life 用 dangerous 加重语气点出\"危险结局\"，是文末\n收束的警示落点。\n【替换】simply drifting through life ↔ Drifting through life aimlessly\nsimply=aimlessly 同义改写\n【排除】B) 未提及\nGiving up the chance to fulfill yourself. 说\"放弃自我实现的机会\"，原文文末讲的是\"漂泊\n度日、无法享受生活\"，并未提到\"放弃自我实现\"，属未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\nAbandoning all that life has to offer. 直接借了原文 all that life has to offer 来诱选，但\n原文是说你\"将无法享受 all that life has to offer\"，是\"无法享受\"而非主动\"abandon（抛\n弃）\"，意思错位。\n借⽤原⽂：enjoy all that life has to offer\nD) 张冠李戴\nSpoiling your character and integrity. 借了第 3 段 character, integrity, and\ntrustworthiness 的词，但那处讲的是\"目标应包含做有品格的人\"这一正面主张，不是\"不追\n目标会毁品格\"的结局，张冠李戴到本题。\n借⽤原⽂：a person of \"good\" character, integrity, and trustworthiness", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker recommend in this talk?", "options": ["A) Grade-raising ways and means.", "B) Pressure-alleviating exercise.", "C) Revision-conducting approaches.", "D) Brain-boosting food and drink."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题） Brain-boosting food and drink.\n【定位】As a nutrition expert, I want to say that eating well can make a real difference to your revision\nregime—so what brain-boosting food and drink would I recommend?\n作为一名营养专家,我想说吃得好能给你的复习状态带来实实在在的改善——那么我会推荐\n哪些健脑的食物和饮品呢?\n【信号】开头自问句直接抛出主题 recommend。讲解者用一句自问 what brain-boosting\nfood and drink would I recommend? 直接亮出全篇主题,recommend 一词与题干\nrecommend 对应,这种开头的点题自问句就是主旨题的答案信号,后面整篇都在回答这个\n问题。\n【替换】what brain-boosting food and drink would I recommend? ↔ Brain-boosting food and drink.\n原文设问中的 brain-boosting food and drink 与选项几乎同形,且后文全篇兑现\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n开头 raise your grades 念到了提高分数,A 项 Grade-raising ways and means. 就盗这个\ngrades 来诱选。但提分只是吃得好带来的结果,全篇真正推荐的是健脑食物饮品,A 把手段说\n成了提分本身,答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：raise your grades\nB) 原词陷阱\n开头 The pressure is on 提到了压力,B 项 Pressure-alleviating exercise. 盗用压力概念并\n改成运动来诱选。但全篇没讲任何运动,讲的全是吃喝,B 既偏离主题又凭空加了运动。\n借⽤原⽂：The pressure is on\nC) 原词陷阱\n开头 It's revision time 与 your revision regime 多次念到 revision,C 项 Revision-\nconducting approaches. 就盗这个 revision 来诱选。但 revision 只是讲话的背景场景,讲\n解者推荐的是辅助复习的饮食,而非怎么开展复习,C 偷换了主题。\n借⽤原⽂：your revision regime", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does the speaker warn against coffee?", "options": ["A) Buying it from coffee shops.", "B) Building up a tolerance of it.", "C) Drinking it after 2 pm.", "D) Consuming it with sugar."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Drinking it after 2 pm.\n【定位】So stop drinking coffee by 2 pm and have a maximum of 2 cups of coffee a day, but be aware\nof your own reaction to it.\n所以下午两点之后就别再喝咖啡了,每天最多喝两杯,但要留意你自己对它的反应。\n【信号】建议句 stop drinking coffee by 2 pm 是直接劝阻信号。讲解者用祈使式建议句 stop\ndrinking coffee by 2 pm,stop…by 是直接劝阻的口吻,正对应题干 warn against(警告别\n做)。这种带 stop/avoid/be aware 的建议句就是细节题里该避免做法的答案信号。\n【替换】stop drinking coffee by 2 pm ↔ Drinking it after 2 pm.\nstop…by 2 pm 等于警告 after 2 pm 别喝,肯否定与时间对照转换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文 coffee from a coffee shop may be stronger than coffee made at home 念到了\ncoffee shop,A 项 Buying it from coffee shops. 就盗这个原词来诱选。但原文只是提醒咖\n啡店的咖啡可能更浓,是一句提示,并没把从咖啡店买列为该警告避免的事,答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：coffee from a coffee shop may be stronger than coffee made at home\nB) 原词陷阱\n原文 individuals can build up a tolerance 念到了 build up a tolerance,B 项 Building\nup a tolerance of it. 直接照搬这个短语来诱选。但原文说的是人会产生耐受、导致提神效\n果短暂,这是在解释咖啡的局限,并不是警告你别去建立耐受(耐受也不是人主动做的事),属答\n非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：individuals can build up a tolerance\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文 Coffee can also increase blood sugar 念到了 sugar,D 项 Consuming it with\nsugar. 就盗这个 sugar 来诱选。但原文说的是咖啡会升高血糖,根本没说要警告别就着糖喝\n咖啡,D 是把 sugar 这个词嫁接到一个原文没说过的做法上,属未提及。\n借⽤原⽂：Coffee can also increase blood sugar", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "How can we get sufficient nutrients according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) By taking varied vitamin supplements.", "B) By consuming a rich variety of foods.", "C) By eating both oranges and frozen berries.", "D) By getting components packaged in tablets."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） By consuming a rich variety of foods.\n【定位】But I would say food should always come before supplements and the key to getting as many\nnutrients as possible is to eat a varied diet with lots of different colors such as frozen berries\nand dark green vegetables.\n但我要说,食物应当永远优先于补充剂,而尽可能多获取营养的关键,在于吃多样化、颜色丰富\n的饮食,比如冷冻莓果和深绿色蔬菜。\n【信号】转折 But + the key 双重信号点明真正途径。讲解者先承认医生常推荐补充剂,随即用 But I\nwould say food should always come before supplements 转折表态,再用 the key to\ngetting as many nutrients as possible is to eat a varied diet 直接给出关键。转折 But\n之后才是真意、加上 the key is 的强调,是细节表态题最硬的答案信号。\n【替换】eat a varied diet with lots of different colors ↔ By consuming a rich variety of foods.\nvaried diet 等于 a rich variety of foods,eat 等于 consuming\nfood should always come before supplements ↔ By consuming a rich variety of foods.\n食物优先于补充剂,正指向靠食物而非补充剂获取营养\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n原文 Doctors often recommend taking vitamin supplements 念到了 vitamin\nsupplements,A 项 By taking varied vitamin supplements. 就盗这个原词来诱选。但那是\n医生常推荐、被讲解者用 But 转折否定的做法,他明说 food should always come before\nsupplements,故靠补充剂正是被驳斥的途径,与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：Doctors often recommend taking vitamin supplements\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文 such as frozen berries 与 oranges contain not only vitamin C 念到了 frozen\nberries 和 oranges,C 项 By eating both oranges and frozen berries. 就把这两个例子拼\n起来诱选。但原文只是用它们举例说明多样饮食,获取营养的途径是吃多样食物,不是单吃这\n两样,C 把举例当成了答案,以偏概全。\n借⽤原⽂：such as frozen berries and dark green vegetables\nD) 与原文相反\n原文 components that you can't get packaged together in a tablet 念到了 packaged\n和 tablet,D 项 By getting components packaged in tablets. 就盗这两个词诱选。但原文\n带 can't,说的是有些成分你没法靠一颗药片打包获得,意思与 D 完全相反,听漏了 can't 就会\n误选。\n借⽤原⽂：components that you can't get packaged together in a tablet", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What is the worrying trend the late Swedish academic has identified?", "options": ["A) Many people across advanced economies think the world is going from bad to worse.", "B) The news focuses on reporting catastrophes that occur across the world.", "C) A significant part of the world is experiencing another great recession.", "D) Many people have no idea of those living under miserable conditions."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\nMany people across advanced economies think the world is going from bad to worse.\n【定位】The late Swedish academic Hans Rosling has identified a worrying trend: not only do many\npeople across advanced economies have no idea that the world is becoming a much better\nplace, but they actually even think the opposite.\n已故瑞典学者汉斯·罗斯林发现了一个令人担忧的趋势：发达经济体里许多人不仅不知道世界\n正在变得好得多，反而恰恰认为情况相反（即觉得世界在变糟）。\n【信号】冒号后直接给出趋势内容。讲者用 has identified a worrying trend: 点题，冒号本身就是\n答案信号——冒号后那句就是“趋势”的具体内容。再加 not only do many people…but\nthey actually even think the opposite 的 not only…but 递进，把“以为世界在变糟”这层\n意思推到最强，正是答案落点。\n【替换】they actually even think the opposite ↔ the world is going from bad to worse\n“想法恰恰相反”=“觉得世界越来越糟”\nmany people across advanced economies ↔ Many people across advanced economies\n主语原样对应\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音确实说了 the news focuses on reporting catastrophes，但那是讲者解释“为什么人\n们会有这种错觉”的原因，不是题干问的“那个令人担忧的趋势本身”。听到 catastrophes 原\n词就选 B，正中圈套。\n借⽤原⽂：the news focuses on reporting catastrophes\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音第 5 段才出现 the recent Great Recession，且是作为长期增长中的一次起落被一笔带\n过，根本不是第 1 段“令人担忧的趋势”。选项 another great recession 借这个眼熟术语骗\n人，与本题答案句无关。\n借⽤原⽂：the recent Great Recession\nD) 张冠李戴\nmiserable conditions 出现在第 4 段 has lived under quite miserable conditions，讲的\n是“历史上很大一部分人曾生活悲惨”，不是“人们没意识到有人过得悲惨”。选项 Many\npeople have no idea of those living under miserable conditions 把第 1 段的 have no\nidea 和第 4 段的 miserable conditions 拼接嫁接，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：has lived under quite miserable conditions", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What does the speaker say about globalization?", "options": ["A) It has experienced ups and downs like any other historical trend.", "B) It is hailed as a miracle by both economists and ordinary people.", "C) It is the only way for all countries to share economic prosperity.", "D) It has given more and more countries a rare chance to thrive."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It is the only way for all countries to share economic prosperity.\n【定位】If we really care about global welfare, globalization is the only way forward to ensure\nthat economic prosperity is shared among all countries and not only a select few\nadvanced economies.\n如果我们真正关心全球福祉，全球化就是确保经济繁荣由所有国家共享、而不只是少数发达\n经济体独享的唯一前进之路。\n【信号】判断句 globalization is the only way。讲者用 globalization is the only way forward\n直接给全球化下定性判断，is the only way 这种“主语＋系动词＋定性”的结构就是答案信\n号。再加后半句 economic prosperity is shared among all countries 点明它指向“让所有\n国家共享繁荣”，与选项口径吻合。\n【替换】globalization is the only way forward ↔ It is the only way\nthe only way 原义对应\neconomic prosperity is shared among all countries ↔\nfor all countries to share economic prosperity\n主被动转换的等值改写\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nups and downs 出现在第 5 段 there were many long-lasting ups and downs，讲的是\n长期增长率有起落，对象是 growth rate，不是题干问的 globalization。选项 It has\nexperienced ups and downs like any other historical trend 张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：there were many long-lasting ups and downs\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音第 5 段说 the constancy of the long-run growth rate is actually quite\nmiraculous，被称为奇迹的是“长期增长率的恒定”，不是全球化，且没有 economists and\nordinary people 一起称颂之说。选项 hailed as a miracle by both economists and\nordinary people 既错对象又无中生有添加了称颂主体。\n借⽤原⽂：the constancy of the long-run growth rate is actually quite miraculous\nD) 偷换概念\n原文说的是 globalization 让繁荣由 all countries（所有国家）共享，是范围最大的表述；\n选项 more and more countries 把‘所有’偷偷缩成‘越来越多’，范围被改小，与讲者口径不\n符。\n借⽤原⽂：shared among all countries", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What do we learn about half of the global population?", "options": ["A) Their fortunes may take a downturn.", "B) They can be classified as middle class.", "C) Their living standards have been deteriorating.", "D) They are experiencing a radical transformation."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They can be classified as middle class.\n【定位】In fact, for the first time ever since the Industrial Revolution, about half of the global\npopulation can be considered middle class.\n事实上，自工业革命以来第一次，大约一半的全球人口可以被视为中产阶级。\n【信号】群体定性句 half…can be considered middle class。末段用 about half of the global\npopulation can be considered middle class 给这群人直接下结论，主语正是题干的 half\nof the global population，谓语 can be considered middle class 就是答案信号。开头的\nIn fact 起强调收束作用，提示这是讲者要落下的结论。\n【替换】can be considered middle class ↔ can be classified as middle class\nconsidered=classified 的同义替换\nabout half of the global population ↔ They\nThey 回指题干群体\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音第 6 段说全球不平等 on a steady downward trend、这群人的 living standards 在\nimprovement，整体是向好的；选项 Their fortunes may take a downturn 说他们境况会\n下行，方向与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：global inequality has been on a steady downward trend\nC) 与原文相反\n录音说 the improvement of hundreds of millions of people's living standards——\nliving standards 是在改善；选项 Their living standards have been deteriorating 盗用\nliving standards 原词却把方向反成 deteriorating（恶化），正负颠倒。\n借⽤原⽂：the improvement of hundreds of millions of people's living standards\nD) 未提及\n录音对这群人只说“可被视为中产”“生活水平改善”，从未说他们正经历 radical\ntransformation（剧变）。选项 They are experiencing a radical transformation 是录音\n里没有的说法，无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What question does the speaker try to answer in this talk?", "options": ["A) Why smells can activate emotional memories.", "B) How powerful the sense of smell can become.", "C) How scent particles revive memories forgotten.", "D) Why the scent of bread has a strong mental impact."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （主旨题） Why smells can activate emotional memories.\n【定位】But why do smells sometimes trigger powerful memories, especially emotional ones? The\nshort answer is that the brain regions that handle smells, memories and emotions are linked.\n可是，为什么气味有时会唤起强烈的记忆，尤其是带情绪的记忆呢？简短的答案是：大脑中\n处理气味、记忆和情绪的区域是相互连接的。\n【信号】转折 But + 自问 + The short answer 收束。第一段先铺垫嗅觉很强，随后用 But why\ndo smells sometimes trigger powerful memories, especially emotional ones? 这个\nBut 引出的设问亮出全文要答的问题，再用 The short answer is 直接给答案；\"转折后自问\n+ 点明答案\"正是篇章主问题的落点信号。\n【替换】why do smells sometimes trigger powerful memories, especially emotional ones ↔\nWhy smells can activate emotional memories.\ntrigger=activate、emotional memories 同指\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nHow powerful the sense of smell can become. 借了开头 the sense of smell is\npowerful 的 powerful，但那只是第一句的引子铺垫，不是全文要回答的问题；全文问的\n是\"为什么\"而非\"嗅觉有多强\"，范围被放大了。\n借⽤原⽂：We all know that the sense of smell is powerful.\nC) 原词陷阱\nHow scent particles revive memories forgotten. 几乎照搬录音 scent particles, in\ngeneral, can revive memories that have been long forgotten，但这句只是引向主问题\n前的过渡陈述、是\"已知现象\"，不是讲者要解答的那个\"为什么\"，属答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：scent particles, in general, can revive memories that have been long forgotten\nD) 原词陷阱\nWhy the scent of bread has a strong mental impact. 抓住开头举的 The delicious\nscent of bread 这个例子，但面包只是说明\"气味像时光机\"的一个例证，全文并非专讲面\n包，以偏概全。\n借⽤原⽂：The delicious scent of bread, for example, can act like a time machine.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What is said to result in an intimate connection between emotions, memories and scents?", "options": ["A) The brain cells' processing of memories experienced as strongly emotional.", "B) The activation of the brain's emotion processing area by chemical particles.", "C) The interaction between chemical particles and the brain cells responsible for smell.", "D) The sensations of scents going directly to the brain's emotional and memory centers."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\nThe sensations of scents going directly to the brain's emotional and memory centers.\n【定位】Scents are the only sensations that travel such a direct path to the emotional and memory\ncenters of the brain. That results in an intimate connection between emotions, memories\nand scents.\n气味是唯一一种沿如此直接的通路抵达大脑情绪中枢和记忆中枢的感觉。正是这一点造成了\n情绪、记忆与气味之间的紧密联系。\n【信号】因果指代 That results in 回指前句。录音用 That results in an intimate connection 中\n的 That 回指紧挨的前一句 Scents are the only sensations that travel such a direct\npath to the emotional and memory centers of the brain；\"That results in\"是明确的因\n果信号，That 指向的那句就是原因，即答案。\n【替换】Scents are the only sensations that travel such a direct path to the emotional and memory centers of\nthe brain\n↔ The sensations of scents going directly to the brain's emotional and memory centers.\ntravel a direct path = going directly\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThe brain cells' processing of memories experienced as strongly emotional. 借了第二\n段 Brain cells then carry that information 的 brain cells，但那是讲气味信息在脑内传递\n的中间步骤，且\"脑细胞处理被强烈情绪体验的记忆\"是生造的说法，并非\"造成紧密联系\"的\n原因。\n借⽤原⽂：Brain cells then carry that information to the area of the brain where emotions\nare processed\nB) 原词陷阱\nThe activation of the brain's emotion processing area by chemical particles. 借了 the\narea of the brain where emotions are processed 和 a chemical particle，但这是描述气\n味信息传到情绪区的过程，不是\"唯一直达通路造成紧密联系\"这个被点名的原因，属答非所\n问。\n借⽤原⽂：A scent is a chemical particle that floats in through the nose\nC) 原词陷阱\nThe interaction between chemical particles and the brain cells responsible for smell.\n把 a chemical particle 与 the region of the brain responsible for smell 的词拼成\"化学\n粒子与嗅觉脑细胞互动\"，录音并未说二者\"互动\"造成紧密联系，是生造关系。\n借⽤原⽂：the region of the brain responsible for smell, where the sensation is first processed", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What does the brain use to give meaning to information and find a memory?", "options": ["A) Imagination.", "B) Association.", "C) Experience.", "D) Context."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Context.\n【定位】If the person had first come across that smell in a very different context, it will be much more\ndifficult to recover the associated memory, because the brain uses the context to give meaning\nto the information and find that memory.\n如果那个人当初是在一个很不一样的情景中第一次接触到这种气味，那么要找回与之相关的\n记忆就会困难得多，因为大脑要靠情景来赋予信息意义、并找到那段记忆。\n【信号】因果 because + 题干原话照应。答案就在 because the brain uses the context to give\nmeaning to the information and find that memory，because 引出原因，且这句与题干\ngive meaning to information and find a memory 几乎一字不差，紧挨 uses 的宾语\ncontext 即为答案。\n【替换】the brain uses the context to give meaning to the information and find that memory ↔ Context.\n原文 the context 直接对应选项 Context\n【排除】A) 未提及\nImagination. 在全文没有任何对应，纯属为凑四项而设的无关词，凭\"嗅觉勾起想象\"的联想\n去选会落空。\nB) 原词陷阱\nAssociation. 借了 the associated memory 里的 associated，但那里 associated 只是修\n饰 memory（相关的记忆），并非大脑\"用来\"赋义的东西，混淆了修饰成分与答案宾语。\n借⽤原⽂：it will be much more difficult to recover the associated memory\nC) 答非所问\nExperience. 很贴合\"嗅觉唤起过往经历\"的常识印象，录音也用过 encountered /\nexperienced 类近义表达，但讲\"赋予意义、找回记忆\"时点名的是 context 不是\nexperience，是常识诱饵。\n借⽤原⽂：a person smells something that's connected to a meaningful event in their past", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What drawback do memories of scents share with other memories?", "options": ["A) Inaccuracy and alterability.", "B) Susceptibility to polar interpretations.", "C) Being personal and individualistic.", "D) Being dependent on relevant scenarios."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Inaccuracy and alterability.\n【定位】The special nature of memories brought back by scents notwithstanding, they have the same\ndrawbacks as other memories. They aren't always accurate, and they can change over time.\n尽管气味唤回的记忆有其特殊性，它们却和其他记忆有着同样的缺点。它们并不总是准确，\n而且会随时间发生改变。\n【信号】让步 notwithstanding 转到 the same drawbacks。末段用 notwithstanding（尽管）\n作让步转折，明确转到 they have the same drawbacks as other memories，紧跟一句把\n缺点摊开：They aren't always accurate, and they can change over time；让步转折后\n的这句就是答案句。\n【替换】They aren't always accurate ↔ Inaccuracy and alterability.\naren't always accurate = Inaccuracy\nthey can change over time ↔ alterability\ncan change over time = alterability\n【排除】B) 过度推断\nSusceptibility to polar interpretations. 像是从 that memory can become distorted 和\nconvinced that the memories are accurate, even when they aren't 引申出的\"易被两极\n解读\"，但录音说的是\"会失真、人却深信准确\"，并没有\"被正反两极解释\"之意，属过度推\n断。\n借⽤原⽂：each time we remember something, that memory can become distorted\nC) 未提及\nBeing personal and individualistic. 符合\"每人气味记忆不同\"的常识，但录音此处讲的\n是\"与其他记忆共有的缺点\"，\"私人化\"既非录音点名的缺点，也不是\"共有\"的特征，与题干\nshare with other memories 相悖。\nD) 张冠李戴\nBeing dependent on relevant scenarios. 把第四段讲的 The explanation is context. 那\n类\"依赖情景\"内容搬到这里，但那是讲\"为何有时想不起记忆\"，不是末段所说的\"共有缺\n点\"，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：because the brain uses the context to give meaning to the information", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affectionate", "B) approximately", "C) elapses", "D) erode", "E) flattered", "F) flips", "G) halt", "H) overburdened", "I) predominates", "J) propagate", "K) scenario", "L) shadow", "M) tentatively", "N) typically", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) shadow\n名词\n【词性槽】「cast a ___ over」固定搭配，宾语位 → 名词\n【依据】cast a shadow over——投下阴影的固定隐喻\nshadow 与 cast ... over 构成 cast a shadow over（给某事投下阴霾），形象表达\nNegative energy（消极情绪）笼罩 efforts to build a shared understanding（构建共识\n的努力），地道贴切。\n【竞争词】K) scenario cast a scenario 不成话\nscenario 表「设想情景」，英语中没有 cast a scenario over 的用法；且消极情绪「投下一个情\n景」逻辑也不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·「cast a ___ over」固定搭"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affectionate", "B) approximately", "C) elapses", "D) erode", "E) flattered", "F) flips", "G) halt", "H) overburdened", "I) predominates", "J) propagate", "K) scenario", "L) shadow", "M) tentatively", "N) typically", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) affectionate\n形容词\n【词性槽】并列名词前的修饰位 → 形容词\n【依据】亲切的微笑，与 warm comments 同色\naffectionate 意为「亲切、充满感情的」，修饰 smiles 是高频搭配（affectionate\nsmile/look），与并列项 warm comments 共同构成积极正能量信号，正切「Sprinkling\nin positivity」主旨。\n【竞争词】E) flattered 对象错位：人才会 flattered，微笑本身不会\nflattered 描述「（人）受宠若惊的」，修饰 smiles 语义不通——是「人对微笑感到受宠若\n惊」，而非「flattered smiles」。\nH) overburdened 感情色彩相反\noverburdened 意为「负担过重的」，是消极色，与「Sprinkling in positivity」整段正向氛围\n冲突。\nO) vigorous 搭配不当：强烈的微笑不地道\nvigorous 意为「强有力的、激烈的」，常修饰 exercise/debate，不修饰 smiles；且与温柔的\nwarm comments 不同色。此处先留作下文 33 题候选。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·并列名词前的修饰位 → 形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affectionate", "B) approximately", "C) elapses", "D) erode", "E) flattered", "F) flips", "G) halt", "H) overburdened", "I) predominates", "J) propagate", "K) scenario", "L) shadow", "M) tentatively", "N) typically", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) flips\n动词\n【词性槽】主句缺谓语，单数主语 → 动词单三\n【依据】flip from X to Y——突然翻转\nflips 表「（状态）突然翻转」，正好对应 from collaborative to adversarial（从协作翻成\n对抗）；与上文 anger erupts（怒气爆发）的「突变」语气吻合。\n【竞争词】C) elapses elapse 只用于「时间流逝」\nelapses 仅与 time/hours/days 搭配表「时间过去」，不接 from...to... 表对话「转变」。\nI) predominates 「占主导」无 from...to... 用法\npredominates 意为「占上风」，是状态而非转变，搭配 from...to... 语义不通。留作 35 题\n候选。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主句缺谓语，单数主语 → 动词单三"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affectionate", "B) approximately", "C) elapses", "D) erode", "E) flattered", "F) flips", "G) halt", "H) overburdened", "I) predominates", "J) propagate", "K) scenario", "L) shadow", "M) tentatively", "N) typically", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) halt\n名词\n【词性槽】「come to a/an ___」固定搭配，宾语位 → 名词\n【依据】come to a halt——立即中止的惯用表达\nhalt 与 come to 构成英语高频搭配 come to a halt（戛然而止）。前文说「dialogue flips\nfrom collaborative to adversarial」，对话转入对抗后，「progress is likely to come to\nan immediate halt」逻辑顺畅。\n【竞争词】K) scenario 「come to a scenario」搭配不存在\nscenario 意为「设想情景」，英语里没有 come to a scenario 这种用法；且 scenario 强调\n「场景设想」，与「progress（进展）」的归宿不搭。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·「come to a/an ___」固定"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affectionate", "B) approximately", "C) elapses", "D) erode", "E) flattered", "F) flips", "G) halt", "H) overburdened", "I) predominates", "J) propagate", "K) scenario", "L) shadow", "M) tentatively", "N) typically", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) typically\n副词\n【词性槽】主谓之间插入副词修饰动词\n【依据】陈述常态规律 → 通常\n本段在讲「不对称话量带来的常见后果」，是一个一般性规律陈述；typically 正好表示「通\n常情况下沉默的一方听久了会厌倦」，与下半句 while the talker can feel __(31)__ 的常态\n描述对仗。\n【竞争词】B) approximately 「大约」用于数量，不修饰动词常态\napproximately 主要用于数字/数量前（approximately 100 people），不用在主谓之间表\n频度。\nM) tentatively 「试探地」与「厌倦」逻辑不搭\ntentatively 表「犹豫地、试探地」，搭 tires of listening 语义不通——人不会「试探地厌\n倦」。留作 34 题候选。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主谓之间插入副词修饰动词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affectionate", "B) approximately", "C) elapses", "D) erode", "E) flattered", "F) flips", "G) halt", "H) overburdened", "I) predominates", "J) propagate", "K) scenario", "L) shadow", "M) tentatively", "N) typically", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) overburdened\n形容词\n【词性槽】feel 后接表语 → 形容词\n【依据】讲个不停的人会感到「负担过重」\n上半句 The silent partner typically tires of only listening, 是沉默方的负面感受；while\n形成对照，话多方一直独挑话题，自然 overburdened（不堪重负）——与下句 Usually,\nboth would prefer more equal give and take. 「希望更对等」呼应。\n【竞争词】E) flattered 感情色彩反了：受宠若惊与抱怨不对\nflattered 是积极感受「受宠若惊」，但下句明确说「双方都希望更对等的 give and take」，说\n明话多方是不满，色彩相反。\nO) vigorous vigorous 不修饰「感觉」\nvigorous 修饰人或活动表「精力充沛」，但 feel vigorous 与上下文「话题不平衡的烦恼」不\n搭。留作 33 题候选。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 E) —— feel 后形容词位 → 容易误选 flattered。词性匹配、又似乎\n「话多的一方受人追捧」逻辑也讲得通，极具迷惑性。防坑：往下读一句 Usually, both\nwould prefer more equal give and take.——既然双方都希望更对等，说明话多方也在抱\n怨，必为负面感受，overburdened 才对。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·feel 后接表语 → 形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affectionate", "B) approximately", "C) elapses", "D) erode", "E) flattered", "F) flips", "G) halt", "H) overburdened", "I) predominates", "J) propagate", "K) scenario", "L) shadow", "M) tentatively", "N) typically", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) erode\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 can 后接动词原形\n【依据】侵蚀对称——负向破坏\nerode 表「逐渐侵蚀、削弱」，宾语 symmetry 正好被「侵蚀」；下文 The faster or\nlouder partner can easily begin taking up more airtime. 印证：语速/音量差异会逐渐打\n破平衡。\n【竞争词】J) propagate 「传播对称」方向反了\npropagate 意为「传播、繁殖」，是正向扩散，与本段「打破对称」的负向逻辑相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·情态动词 can 后接动词原形"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affectionate", "B) approximately", "C) elapses", "D) erode", "E) flattered", "F) flips", "G) halt", "H) overburdened", "I) predominates", "J) propagate", "K) scenario", "L) shadow", "M) tentatively", "N) typically", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) vigorous\n形容词\n【词性槽】more + 空 — 形容词比较级位\n【依据】更铿锵有力的表达风格\nvigorous 修饰 style of expression 表「铿锵有力、强势」，与 One person's input may\ncarry more sway（更具影响力）形成因果——表达风格更有力，因此话语权更大。\n【竞争词】E) flattered 受宠若惊的表达不通\nflattered 描述人的情绪「受宠若惊」，不能修饰 style of expression；且语义与「话语权更\n大」无因果关联。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·more + 空 — 形容词比较级位"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affectionate", "B) approximately", "C) elapses", "D) erode", "E) flattered", "F) flips", "G) halt", "H) overburdened", "I) predominates", "J) propagate", "K) scenario", "L) shadow", "M) tentatively", "N) typically", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) tentatively\n副词\n【词性槽】more + 空，修饰动词 expressed → 副词\n【依据】试探性地表达 → 容易被忽视\ntentatively 表「试探性地、犹豫地」，与上文 vigorous（强有力）形成鲜明对照：一\n方表达强势，另一方表达试探——所以 One viewpoint may tend to get lost（观点被\n忽视）。\n【竞争词】B) approximately approximately 用于数量，不修饰表达方式\napproximately 表「大约、近似」，搭配数字/数量；说话「大约地表达」不通，也无法解释观\n点为何「被忽视」。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 B) —— 副词位 → 容易误选 approximately。看到 more + 空就抓\n副词，approximately 是副词没错，但它只搭数字。防坑：把 expressed more\napproximately 整句读出来——「更近似地表达」逻辑上无法解释「观点被忽视」，反义对\n照才合理，tentatively 才是答案。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·more + 空，修饰动词 expres"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affectionate", "B) approximately", "C) elapses", "D) erode", "E) flattered", "F) flips", "G) halt", "H) overburdened", "I) predominates", "J) propagate", "K) scenario", "L) shadow", "M) tentatively", "N) typically", "O) vigorous"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) predominates\n动词\n【词性槽】if 从句缺谓语，单数主语 → 动词单三\n【依据】占主导 vs 隐没——本段对照的两极\npredominates 表「占主导、压过另一方」，与并列项 evaporates（消失、隐没）正好是\n一对反义对照：一方持续占上风、另一方消失，呼应前文「话量不对称」的整体主题。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·if 从句缺谓语，单数主语 → 动词单三"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "We cannot see the moon at times when it is positioned right between the earth and the sun.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】月亮卡在地日之间不可见\n【定位】It's invisible to us during the new moon, because our satellite is parked right between us and\nthe sun; the so-called dark side of the moon is lit up like Las Vegas, but the side we can see is\nin shadow.\n新月时月亮对我们是不可见的，因为我们的卫星正停在我们和太阳之间；月球所谓的“暗面”\n被照得像拉斯维加斯一样亮，而我们能看到的那一面则处在阴影中。\n【改写】We cannot see the moon ↔ It's invisible to us\n看不见 = 不可见\nwhen it is positioned right between the earth and the sun ↔\nour satellite is parked right between us and the sun\n位于=停在，月=卫星", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Volcanic eruptions may cause the moon to assume a moody blue color.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】火山喷发与忧郁蓝色月亮\n【定位】A moon can indeed take on a moody blue color, but this only happens when particles of just\nthe right size disperse through the sky—and it has nothing to do with the moon's status as\n\"blue\". Big clouds of ash from volcanic eruptions or fires can do the trick, but it doesn't\nhappen often, and the stars would certainly have to align for two such rare instances to occur\nat once.\n月亮确实可以呈现一种忧郁的蓝色，但这种情况只发生在合适尺寸的颗粒在天空中扩散的时\n候——和月亮的“蓝月”身份毫无关系。火山爆发或火灾产生的大片灰云能做到这一点，但这\n种情况并不常发生，要两件如此罕见的事同时出现真得天时地利人和。\n【改写】Volcanic eruptions ↔ Big clouds of ash from volcanic eruptions or fires\n术语原样复现\nmay cause the moon to assume a moody blue color ↔ can do the trick\n动词短语承接前句指代", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "The moon will be closest to the earth when the supermoon occurs in February.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】2 月超级月亮最近地球\n【定位】January's super blood wolf moon eclipse is super because the date lines up with the closest\nthe moon will get to us during January, but the moon won't actually be at its closest for the\nyear until February's supermoon, which you can expect plenty of undue excitement over.\n1 月的超级血狼月食之所以“超级”，是因为日期对上了月亮在 1 月里离我们最近的时刻，但\n月亮要到 2 月的超级月亮时才真正达到全年最近的距离，到时你又能预期一波过度的兴奋。\n【改写】The moon will be closest to the earth ↔ the moon won't actually be at its closest for the year\nnot until=才 的逻辑反转\nwhen the supermoon occurs in February ↔ February's supermoon\n名词所属结构 → 时间从句\n【辨邻】 F F 段 2018's closest perigee and most distant apogee both happened in January\n把最近距离锁在 1 月，与陈述句要的“2 月最近”恰好相反；F 段是话题相邻段（都讲距\n离），但并没有承载本句信息。\n2018's closest perigee and most distant apogee both happened in January", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "There is nothing unusual about the super blood wolf moon, which will bear no resemblance to a bloody wolf head.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】超级血狼月与血淋狼头\n【定位】As we'll explain below, there is nothing truly mysterious or otherwise special about this\nmoon, and it will not look like a bloody wolf head in the sky or anything wild like that.\n我们下面会解释，关于这个月亮其实没有任何真正神秘或特别之处，它也不会在天上看起来\n像一个血淋淋的狼头或别的什么疯狂的样子。\n【改写】There is nothing unusual about the super blood wolf moon ↔\nthere is nothing truly mysterious or otherwise special about this moon\nunusual=mysterious or special\nwhich will bear no resemblance to a bloody wolf head ↔\nit will not look like a bloody wolf head in the sky\nbear no resemblance to=will not look like", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "The moon will appear orange when the light reflecting from it travels through an atmosphere thicker than usual.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】更厚大气与橙色月亮\n【定位】The moon can also look orange whenever it's rising or setting, or if it hangs low in the\nhorizon all night—the light bouncing off of it has to travel through a thicker atmosphere\nthere, which scatters more blue light away.\n月亮在升起或落下时，或者整夜挂在地平线附近时，也会看上去呈橙色——因为从它反射出\n去的光必须穿过那里更厚的大气层，而较厚的大气层会把更多的蓝光散射掉。\n【改写】The moon will appear orange ↔ The moon can also look orange\nappear=look\nwhen the light reflecting from it travels through an atmosphere thicker than usual ↔\nthe light bouncing off of it has to travel through a thicker atmosphere there\nreflecting=bouncing off, thicker than usual=thicker", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "It is contrary to logic to claim the name of wolf moon originates from the cultures of American Indians.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】狼月名字源自美洲印第安\n【定位】Lots of websites will tell you that \"wolf moon\" is the traditional name of the first full moon of\nthe year in \"Native American\" cultures, which is kind of a weird thing to claim given that\nthere are 573 registered Tribal Nations in the US alone today, not to mention historically. The\nidea that hungry, howling wolves were such a universal constant in January that all of North\nAmerica with its different cultures, geographies, and languages spontaneously came up with\nthe same nickname is illogical.\n很多网站会告诉你“狼月”是“美洲原住民”文化中一年第一次满月的传统名字，但这种说法挺\n奇怪——光今日美国就有 573 个注册部落，更别提历史上的部落了。“饥饿、嗥叫的狼”在 1\n月是这样一种放之北美而皆准的常量，以至于不同文化、地理、语言的整个北美都自发想出\n同一昵称——这种说法不合逻辑。\n【改写】It is contrary to logic to claim ↔ is illogical\ncontrary to logic=illogical\nthe name of wolf moon originates from the cultures of American Indians ↔\n“wolf moon” is the traditional name of the first full moon of the year in “Native American”\ncultures\nAmerican Indians = Native American", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "As the moon's orbit is not a hundred-percent circle, its distance from the earth changes.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】月亮轨道非完美圆形\n【定位】The moon isn't always exactly the same distance from Earth, because its orbit isn't\nperfectly circular.\n月亮和地球的距离并不总是一模一样，因为它的轨道并非完全是圆形。\n【改写】the moon's orbit is not a hundred-percent circle ↔ its orbit isn't perfectly circular\nhundred-percent=perfectly\nits distance from the earth changes ↔\nThe moon isn't always exactly the same distance from Earth\n距离变化 = 距离不总相同", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "A full moon in a given month or season gets a specific name in different cultures.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】不同文化给某月满月起名\n【定位】Many cultures have traditional names for the full moon in a given month or season, so there's\nquite a list to draw from if you're trying to really juice up a story on a slightly-bigger-than-\naverage view of the moon.\n许多文化对特定月份或季节的满月都有传统名字，所以如果你想给一篇关于“略大于平均”的\n月亮观感的报道加点料，可选的名字相当多。\n【改写】A full moon in a given month or season ↔ the full moon in a given month or season\n限定结构原样复现\ngets a specific name in different cultures ↔ Many cultures have traditional names for\n主谓换序的等价改写\n【辨邻】 M M 段 wolf moon 是单一例子的反例，并非总括“许多文化对某月/某季满月起名”这条\n事实；总括段是 N。\n“wolf moon” is the traditional name of the first full moon of the year in “Native American” cultures", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "There are likely lots of people who know absolutely nothing about why the moon appears to change its size regularly but act like they know.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】假装懂月相变化\n【定位】There are probably loads of folks who walk around pretending they totally know why that\nthing in the sky seems to get bigger and smaller at regular intervals but who totally do not.\n可能有一大堆人到处走来走去，假装自己完全知道天上那玩意儿为什么看上去会有规律地变\n大变小，但其实完全不知道。\n【改写】lots of people who know absolutely nothing ↔\nloads of folks who walk around pretending they totally know...but who totally do not\nlots=loads, people=folks\nwhy the moon appears to change its size regularly ↔\nwhy that thing in the sky seems to get bigger and smaller at regular intervals\nthe moon=that thing in the sky\nact like they know ↔ pretending they totally know\nact like=pretend\n【辨邻】 E E 段是科学解释段，回答“月相为何变化”，但本句要的是 act like they know 这条人物\n画像——E 段没有装懂者的描写。\nAnd all the other phases are just the transition from one of those extremes to the other", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "When a full moon appears twice in the same month, it is nicknamed a blue moon.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】一月两次满月叫蓝月\n【定位】And while that's not necessarily special in an oh-gosh-get-out-and-look-at-it way, it's\ncertainly special: a blue moon is a nickname for when two full moons fall in the same\ncalendar month, and we hadn't previously had two in one year since 1999.\n尽管这并不属于“哦天哪赶紧出门看”那种特别，但它确实算特别：蓝月是给“同一个日历月里\n出现两次满月”这种情况起的昵称，而我们上一次在一年里出现两次蓝月还是 1999 年。\n【改写】When a full moon appears twice in the same month ↔\nwhen two full moons fall in the same calendar month\n数词分布的等价改写\nit is nicknamed a blue moon ↔ a blue moon is a nickname for\n定义方向反转\n【辨邻】 K K 段 not actually blue 与 moody blue color 都谈“蓝月颜色”，并不给“蓝月”的命名定\n义；定义段是 J。\nAlso, fun fact: not actually blue\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA\n开篇用调侃口吻列举各种月亮名号，吐槽网络媒体热衷炒作 Blue moons, rose moons,\nsupermoons，并并列两种相反的标题论调，引出全文要讨论的对象——网上流传的各\n种“月亮事件”。\n信息指纹 媒体炒作的反差标题\nB 点出全文核心对象——1 月 20 日的“超级血狼月食”，并明确说它没有任何神秘特别之\n处，也不会像血淋淋的狼头 it will not look like a bloody wolf head in the sky。\n信息指纹 1 月 20 日的超级血狼月食\nC 一句过渡，宣称本文是“赏月新闻一站式资源”，承上启下，无实质信息。\n信息指纹 一站式资源的过渡句\nD\n承认有“一大堆人”其实根本不知道月亮为何看上去周期性变大变小 that thing in the\nsky seems to get bigger and smaller at regular intervals，却假装自己懂。\n信息指纹 假装懂月相变化的人\nE\n讲月相成因：月亮被潮汐锁定永远展示同一面；新月时月亮处在我们和太阳之间 our\nsatellite is parked right between us and the sun 所以看不见；满月时地球处在中间，\n我们能看到被照亮的那面。\n信息指纹 新月时月亮卡在地日之间\nF 解释月地距离会变：因为月亮轨道不是完美圆形 its orbit isn't perfectly circular，所以\n有近地点（perigee）和远地点（apogee），2018 年这两个极值相差约 3 万英里。\n信息指纹 近地点 perigee 与远地点 apogee\nG 说明为什么这个距离变化值得关心——它把满月变成“超级月亮”：满月发生在\n近地点附近时看起来更大；本月血狼月食“超级”是因日期对上 1 月最近距，但\n全年最近要等 2 月 the moon won't actually be at its closest for the year until\nFebruary's supermoon。\n信息指纹 2 月才是全年最近的超级月亮\nH\n讲血月与月亮变橙：血月只在月全食时出现，月亮滑过地球阴影时被染红；月亮升落\n或贴近地平线时也会显橙色，因为反射光要穿过更厚的大气层 the light bouncing off\nof it has to travel through a thicker atmosphere there，散射掉更多蓝光。\n信息指纹 更厚大气层造成的橙色月亮\nI 评论性段：大多数关于月亮的标题都很可笑，但血月真值得看一眼；它只是月食而已\nthey're just lunar eclipses，不是天神间血战的征兆。\n信息指纹 血月只是月食，值得起床看\nJ 讲 2018 年的第二次“蓝月”：蓝月 = 同一日历月内出现两次满月的昵称 a blue moon is\na nickname for when two full moons fall in the same calendar month；上一次一年两\n次蓝月是 1999 年，下一次要等 2037 年；并解释月历与月相周期不同步的成因。\n信息指纹 蓝月 = 同月两次满月的昵称\nK\n讲“蓝月”其实不蓝：月亮真要呈现忧郁的蓝色，得靠合适尺寸的颗粒在天空中扩散\n——火山爆发或火灾产生的大片灰云能做到这事 Big clouds of ash from volcanic\neruptions or fires can do the trick，但很罕见。\n信息指纹 火山灰让月亮真变蓝\nL\n顺带提“复活节满月（Paschal moon）”——就是春天的第一次满月，常用来定复活节\n日期；作者表态拒绝深入这套“日历瞎扯”。\n信息指纹 Paschal moon 与复活节\nM\n吐槽“狼月”叫法：网站说“wolf moon”是美洲原住民传统名，但全美光今日就有 573 个\n注册部落 there are 573 registered Tribal Nations in the US alone today，文化、地\n理、语言各异，却同时想出同一昵称——不合逻辑 is illogical。\n信息指纹 狼月叫法源自美洲原住民的逻辑漏洞\nN 总结：许多文化都给特定月份或季节的满月起了传统名字 Many cultures have\ntraditional names for the full moon in a given month or season；但这些都基于人类\n历法、活动和民俗，不会真去看到“粉红月亮”。\n信息指纹 各文化给特定月份满月的传统名", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What has become of the rural areas nowadays?", "options": ["A) They are becoming somewhat deserted.", "B) They are being increasingly urbanised.", "C) They are closely-knit communities.", "D) They are haunted by conservatism."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What has become of the rural areas nowadays? 问的是「现在乡村怎么样了」，要的是\n客观状态描述，不含 infer/suggest/imply。\n【拆句】What has become of the rural areas nowadays? 是常规疑问句，become of 是固定搭\n配，意为「（某事物）变成了什么样、下场如何」，整句直译为「乡村现在变成什么样\n了」，无陷阱。\n【定位】Attracted by the improved economics of urban areas, country-dwellers across the world are\nmoving out, contributing to the excessive crowdedness of densely populated cities, and\nleaving the countryside in desolation.\n受城市改善的经济条件吸引，世界各地的乡村居民正在外迁，加剧了人口稠密城市的过度拥\n挤，并使乡村陷入荒凉。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nleaving the countryside in desolation ↔ They are becoming somewhat deserted.\ncountry-dwellers across the world are moving out ↔ They are becoming somewhat deserted.\nA 把「正在外流→正变得荒凉」浓缩成 becoming somewhat deserted，与预判完全\n一致；somewhat 还为「程度词」留了余地，避免被「过于绝对」反驳。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文讲的是 contributing to the excessive crowdedness of densely populated cities\n——人去了城市，乡村反而空了；选项 They are being increasingly urbanised. 却说\n乡村正被「城市化」，方向完全相反。\nC) 张冠李戴\nRural life is associated with closely-knit communities 说的是乡村生活「被联想为」\n紧密社区，是一种「印象」，并非 nowadays 的现实；选项 They are closely-knit\ncommunities. 把印象当现状。\nD) 以偏概全\nso do conservatism and a lack of employment opportunities 把 conservatism 与\n「就业不足」并列为问题，原文紧接着说 And it is mostly due to the latter that rural\ncommunities are suffering——主因是后者（就业），并非保守主义；D 把次要因素夸\n大为现状。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the author think of the rural lifestyle?", "options": ["A) It is unlikely to survive given the on-going changes.", "B) It is less energy-efficient compared to that in cities.", "C) It is highly dependent upon heavy automobiles.", "D) It is a choice more affluent people will endorse."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author think of the rural lifestyle? 问作者对乡村生活方式的明确观点，\n答案是原文直接写出的评价，并未要求做逻辑推断。\n【拆句】What does the author think of the rural lifestyle? 是固定句型，think of=「对……的看\n法」；直译「作者怎么看乡村生活方式」，无陷阱。\n【定位】We may be piled up like boxes in the city, but this brings energy efficiency that the\ncountryside cannot match. Rural energy requirements result in higher carbon emissions per\nperson than in the city, so it is actually irresponsible to endorse such a lifestyle choice.\n我们在城市里也许像盒子一样堆在一起，但这带来了乡村无法企及的能效。乡村的能耗导致\n人均碳排比城市更高，因此推崇这种生活方式实际上是不负责任的。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nP3 评价乡村生活方式不可取，但并未说它「无法存活」；It is unlikely to survive\ngiven the on-going changes. 借用了 how can the countryside ensure its survival\n里的 survival，却把「能否生存」的话题误植到「作者对生活方式的判断」上。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthis brings energy efficiency that the countryside cannot match ↔\nIt is less energy-efficient compared to that in cities.\nhigher carbon emissions per person than in the city ↔\nIt is less energy-efficient compared to that in cities.\n整段评价的核心证据就是「乡村能效低、碳排高」，B 一字不差地复述了这层比较关\n系，且严格限定在「能效」维度，与预判一致。\nC) 偷换概念\nthose living in the countryside are heavier automobile users 说的是「乡村人是更\n重度的汽车使用者」（使用频率/比例更高），选项 It is highly dependent upon\nheavy automobiles. 把 heavier 移植到 automobiles 之前，变成「依赖重型汽车」，\n偷换了 heavier 的修饰对象。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文 it is actually irresponsible to endorse such a lifestyle choice 直接评价「推崇这\n种生活方式是不负责任的」，作者持反对态度；选项 It is a choice more affluent\npeople will endorse. 不仅没指出作者的反对，反而把「有钱人会推崇」当成结论。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does the author think of rich urban residents buying a second home in the countryside?", "options": ["A) It is bound to mar the traditional rural life.", "B) It adversely impacts rural social stability.", "C) It hinders rural economies' revitalisation.", "D) It is detrimental to rural preservation."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author think of rich urban residents buying a second home in the\ncountryside? 问的是作者对这一具体行为的明确评价，答案出现在原文同一段的总结句。\n【拆句】rich urban residents buying a second home in the countryside 中 buying 是现在分词\n作 residents 的后置定语，相当于「买第二套乡村房的城市富人」；定语稍长但不绕。\n【定位】A solution has yet to be found for this challenge, for market forces are further forcing\nresidents out of the countryside. For those who believe in rural preservation, this\nis deplorable.\n这一难题尚无解决方案，因为市场力量正在进一步把居民赶出乡村。对那些信奉乡村保护的\n人而言，这是令人痛心的。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nP4 谈的是房价与本地居民问题，并未提及 traditional rural life 是否被破坏；考生易把\n第二段提到的「亲密社区/简单生活」的传统印象嫁接到此，但原文未在 P4 谈这一面。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 house prices are pushed up... leaving them unaffordable for local people 谈\n的是房价与可负担性，并未谈 rural social stability；选项把经济层面问题升格成「社会\n稳定」这种更大的范畴。\nC) 无中生有\nP4 谈的是市场力量与房价，并未提及 rural economies' revitalisation；revitalisation\n这一话题在 P6 rural renaissance 处才隐约出现，与 P4 的论点无关。\nD) ✓ 正确\nFor those who believe in rural preservation, this is deplorable ↔\nIt is detrimental to rural preservation.\nleaving them unaffordable for local people ↔ It is detrimental to rural preservation.\n原文连续给出「房价上涨→本地人买不起→市场力量进一步驱赶居民→对乡村保护者而\n言可悲」四步推理，落脚点正是「对乡村保护不利」；D 既保留了评价的负面性，又精\n准落到 preservation 这个对象上。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What do we learn about the countryside with industrialisation and technological advances?", "options": ["A) It is destined to change gradually.", "B) It will present an idealised way of life.", "C) It is likely to embrace foreign cultures.", "D) It will become increasingly diversified."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about the countryside with industrialisation and technological\nadvances? 问从文中得知什么，答案直接来自原文表述，不要求二步推断。\n【拆句】the countryside with industrialisation and technological advances 中 with 引出伴随状\n语，意为「在工业化与技术进步的影响下」，with 短语作 countryside 的后置修饰，整句\n直译「在工业化与技术进步下，我们对乡村得知什么」。\n【定位】Whilst an idealised view of the countryside is common, the reality is that rural life has\ncontinuously evolved. Industrialisation brought an increase in divisions of labour, changing\nsocial links between rural people. The internet has introduced once foreign cultures into the\ncountryside. Improved travel connections and affordability have made the world more\naccessible, creating a \"global village\". Considering these changes, surely an evolving\ncountryside is inevitable.\n尽管对乡村理想化的印象很常见，现实却是乡村生活一直在持续演变。工业化带来分工增\n加，改变了乡村人之间的社会联系。互联网把曾经陌生的文化带进了乡村。交通改善与可承\n受让世界更触手可及，造就了「地球村」。鉴于这些变化，乡村必然在演变之中。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nsurely an evolving countryside is inevitable ↔ It is destined to change gradually.\nrural life has continuously evolved ↔ It is destined to change gradually.\nP5 段尾既明示必然性（inevitable）又通过 continuously evolved 暗示渐进性，A 把\n这两层语义都包含进 destined to change gradually，与预判完全一致。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文 Whilst an idealised view of the countryside is common, the reality is that\nrural life has continuously evolved 用 whilst 让步、the reality is 转折，明确把「理\n想化乡村」当成被否定的印象；选项 It will present an idealised way of life. 反把它说\n成将要呈现的现实。\nC) 以偏概全\nThe internet has introduced once foreign cultures into the countryside 只是段中\n三件平行例证之一，与「分工」「全球村」共同支撑「乡村必然演变」这一论点；选项\nIt is likely to embrace foreign cultures. 把单一例子当成结论。\nD) 过度推断\ncreating a \"global village\" 强调「世界更可及、变成地球村」，并不等于乡村会「越来\n越多元化」；选项 It will become increasingly diversified. 在「多文化进入」之上多走\n一步，推出 diversified 这个原文未说的结论。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author think of the future of the countryside compared with cities?", "options": ["A) It is going to be harder for the locals.", "B) It is going to resemble that of cities.", "C) It is less likely to stay static.", "D) It is less easy to envisage."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author think of the future of the countryside compared with cities?\n问作者对未来的对比判断，原文末段直接给出明示对比，不需推断。\n【拆句】What does the author think of the future of the countryside compared with cities?\n中 compared with cities 是过去分词短语作 future 的后置定语，意为「与城市相比的乡村\n的未来」；句子结构稍长但语义直白。\n【定位】We know that the world is going to see new cities emerging from the dust and current cities\ngrowing into megacities, but the future of the countryside is less clear.\n我们知道，世界将看到新城从尘土中崛起、现有城市发展为超大型城市，但乡村的未来则不\n那么清晰。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nP7 未提及 harder for the locals；本地人变难的话题在 P4 leaving them\nunaffordable for local people 出现过，但与「未来对比」无关；选项把跨段细节挪过\n来冒充末段对比的结论。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文 but the future of the countryside is less clear 把乡村未来与城市未来拉开差\n距；选项 It is going to resemble that of cities. 反说「乡村未来会像城市的未来」，把\n对比关系反转为类似关系。\nC) 答非所问\nBut change is inevitable 谈的是变化必然，It is less likely to stay static. 复述这一变\n化必然性；但本题问的是「乡村未来与城市未来相比」这一对比维度，C 答的是「乡村\n是否会变」的另一维度。\nD) ✓ 正确\nbut the future of the countryside is less clear ↔ It is less easy to envisage.\nWe know that the world is going to see new cities emerging from the dust and current cities\ngrowing into megacities\n↔ It is less easy to envisage.\n对比型细节题正确项必须同时保留比较关系与比较内容；D 既保留 less，又把 clear 同\n义置换为 easy to envisage，与预判完全契合。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the passage say about the statement \"beauty is in the eye of the beholder\"?", "options": ["A) It has hardly ever been disputed.", "B) It can be interpreted aesthetically.", "C) Many people have found it absurd.", "D) People have long been misled by it."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the passage say about the statement 把范围圈死在那句引语上，要求\n复述作者对它的具体描述，不是推断也不是主旨。\n【拆句】What does the passage say about the statement \"beauty is in the eye of the\nbeholder\"? 主干是 What does the passage say about X，X = the statement \"beauty\nis in the eye of the beholder\"；structure 平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Statements, like \"beauty is in the eye of the beholder(观看者)\", are rarely questioned.\n诸如「美在观看者的眼中」这样的说法，几乎从不受到质疑。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nrarely questioned ↔ hardly ever been disputed\n预判「几乎无人质疑」与 hardly ever been disputed 在频率与语义上完全等值，锁\n定 A。\nB) 偷换概念\nIt can be interpreted aesthetically 说「它可被审美地解读」，与原文 rarely\nquestioned 完全无关；作者只说「无人质疑」，没说「可被审美解读」。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文说「人们觉得 absurd to think otherwise」（反着想才荒谬），而 Many people\nhave found it absurd 把「荒谬」直接扣给那句谚语本身，正话反说。\nD) 无中生有\nPeople have long been misled by it 说「人们长期被它误导」，原文只说作者「想\npush back 一下」，并未给该谚语贴「误导」标签。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does a recent study exploring aesthetic taste show?", "options": ["A) Aesthetic tastes tend to differ from person to person.", "B) Natural beauty is rarely surpassed by artificial beauty.", "C) There is less consensus on objects shaped by human craft.", "D) There is general agreement on what is pleasing to the eye."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does a recent study exploring aesthetic taste show 问的是一项具体研究 show\n出什么——属于事实复述。\n【拆句】What does a recent study exploring aesthetic taste show? 主干是 What does a study\nshow；exploring aesthetic taste 是 study 的现在分词后置定语，作用是限定哪项研究。\n结构清晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】The results from this study show that people agree very much in their aesthetic evaluation of\nnatural objects, but they disagree more about artifacts, or human-made objects.\n这项研究的结果显示，人们对自然物的审美评价高度一致，但对人造物（即人工制造的物\n品）则分歧更多。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nAesthetic tastes tend to differ from person to person 把原文「人造物上分歧」扩大\n成「审美口味本来就因人而异」，丢掉了原文「自然物上一致」这一半。\nB) 偷换概念\nNatural beauty is rarely surpassed by artificial beauty 把研究讲成「自然美几乎不被\n人造美超越」，原文谈的是「评价一致性」，不是「美的优劣比较」。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthey disagree more about artifacts, or human-made objects ↔\nless consensus on objects shaped by human craft\n预判要求选项聚焦「人造物上分歧更多」，less consensus on objects shaped by\nhuman craft 完整对应该语义，锁 C。\nD) 以偏概全\nThere is general agreement on what is pleasing to the eye 只说「人们对什么好看\n普遍一致」，丢掉了原文「对人造物分歧更多」这关键一半，与研究的对比结论不符。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "Why do people differ in their aesthetic appraisal of artifacts?", "options": ["A) They come out in a great variety of designs.", "B) They lead to varying behavioral consequences.", "C) They take up much less natural space than landscapes.", "D) They come into people's view less often than natural spaces."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】Why do people differ in their aesthetic appraisal of artifacts 问的是一项已写明的具体\n原因，不是让你推断作者的言外之意。\n【拆句】Why do people differ in their aesthetic appraisal of artifacts? 一般疑问句结构 Why do\nS V O，differ in 是固定搭配（在某方面有差异）；无陷阱。\n【定位】Art and architecture, unlike natural spaces, do not have the same level of exposure. So,\npeople do not have the same level of shared taste.\n艺术与建筑（不像自然空间）没有同等程度的曝光度。因此，人们对它们的共同审美趣味也\n达不到同样的程度。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nThey come out in a great variety of designs 谈的是「设计多样」，与原文给出的原\n因 exposure 完全无关；这是 P9 里 a wide array of embodiments 的话被移花接木\n过来。\nB) 与原文相反\nP6 明说差异 has to do more with elements of style, rather than \"behavioral\nconsequences\"——作者用 rather than 否定了 behavioral consequences；They\nlead to varying behavioral consequences 反而把被否定的那一支抬回来当原因。\nC) 无中生有\nThey take up much less natural space than landscapes 说人造物比自然景观「占\n用的自然空间更少」，原文从未做过这种空间面积比较；这是按生活常识替原文补造的\n对比。\nD) ✓ 正确\ndo not have the same level of exposure ↔\ncome into people's view less often than natural spaces\n预判要求「人造物被看到的机会少」，与 come into people's view less often than\nnatural spaces 完全等值，锁 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the example of sunset tell about nature?", "options": ["A) It creates a powerful appeal through its brilliant radiance.", "B) It displays aesthetic traits in a similar way the world over.", "C) It embodies beauty with a wide array of aesthetic features.", "D) It establishes the principles of beauty in a number of ways."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （例证目的题）\n【判型】What does the example of sunset tell about nature 问的是举这个例子是为了告诉读者\n关于 nature 的什么——典型的「例证目的」考法。\n【拆句】What does the example of sunset tell about nature? 主干 What does X tell about Y；\ntell 在此处意为 reveal / indicate（说明、揭示），不要按字面理解为「告诉某人某事」。\n【定位】Nature exhibits some of the universal aesthetic features, such as radiance, in a common way\nthroughout the world. It's not exactly the same everywhere, but it is common. For example, a\nsunset is similar enough in different places to warrant almost universal appeal.\n自然界以共通的方式在世界各地呈现某些普遍的审美特征（比如光辉）。各处并不完全相\n同，但确实有共通之处。例如，落日在不同地方足够相似，因而几乎能引起普世的吸引力。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nIt creates a powerful appeal through its brilliant radiance 谈的是「落日靠光辉本身\n创造强烈吸引力」，是 sunset 例子的表层描写，不是例子要说明的论点（共通性）。\nB) ✓ 正确\nNature exhibits some of the universal aesthetic features, such as radiance, in a common way\nthroughout the world\n↔ It displays aesthetic traits in a similar way the world over\n预判要求「自然在全球以共通方式呈现审美特征」，It displays aesthetic traits in a\nsimilar way the world over 与论点句逐项匹配，锁 B。\nC) 张冠李戴\nIt embodies beauty with a wide array of aesthetic features 借用 a wide array of\nembodiments 与 Possible universal principles of beauty 的短语，但 P9 谈的是「美\n的原则允许多样体现」，不是 sunset 例子要说明的内容。\nD) 过度推断\nIt establishes the principles of beauty in a number of ways 把「sunset 例证一种共\n通呈现方式」夸大成「确立了多个美学原则」，原文从未把 sunset 提到「确立原则」的\n高度。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "例证目的题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "Why do artifacts not always hold universal appeal?", "options": ["A) The appreciation of their beauty is not subject to objective standards.", "B) The universal principle of beauty prevents a wide array of embodiments.", "C) The way their creators apply the principle of beauty may not be pleasing to everybody.", "D) Their creators interpret the universal principle of beauty each in their preferred way."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】Why do artifacts not always hold universal appeal 仍是对已写明原因的复述问法，作者\n已经直接写出「为什么」，不是推断。\n【拆句】Why do artifacts not always hold universal appeal? 否定副词 not always 表示「并非总\n是」（部分否定），不是「从来不」；不要错读成 never。\n【定位】When people get involved in making artifacts in architecture or art, they apply these very\ngeneral concepts in unique ways. But the way they applied the principle may not have\nuniversal appeal.\n当人们投身于建筑或艺术中的人造物创作时，他们以独特的方式运用这些非常宽泛的概念。\n但他们运用原则的方式未必能引起所有人的共鸣。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nThe appreciation of their beauty is not subject to objective standards 说「人造物\n之美不受客观标准约束」，但作者在 P7 与 P9 都坚持存在 objective ground /\nPossible universal principles of beauty，A 与作者立场相反。\nB) 与原文相反\nThe universal principle of beauty prevents a wide array of embodiments 说原则\n「阻止」多样体现，原文恰恰说 are general categories, which allow for a wide\narray of embodiments——「允许」多样体现。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe way they applied the principle may not have universal appeal ↔\nThe way their creators apply the principle of beauty may not be pleasing to everybody\n「运用方式」+「未必人人喜欢」两个语义落点在 The way their creators apply the\nprinciple of beauty may not be pleasing to everybody 都得到对应，锁 C。\nD) 以偏概全\nTheir creators interpret the universal principle of beauty each in their preferred\nway 只说「创作者各自按偏好诠释原则」，并未传达「未必人人喜欢」这层「为何没有\n普遍吸引力」的因果；它只复述了 they apply these very general concepts in\nunique ways 的前半段，丢掉了 But 之后的关键否定。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
